of juvenile candidates [1]; and **human genetics**, where polygenic risk scores stratify disease risk for conditions such as coronary artery disease and type 2 diabetes, potentially enabling earlier screening and preventive intervention [3][5].\n\n**Key methodological components.** (1) A **training (reference) population** — individuals with both high-quality phenotypes and genome-wide genotypes, from which the model learns marker–trait relationships; a larger, more representative training population generally yields higher accuracy [1]. (2) A **validation population** — separate or held-out individuals used to test model performance, typically via cross-validation [1]. (3) **Statistical/machine learning models** — common approaches include genomic best linear unbiased prediction (GBLUP) and Bayesian methods that treat marker effects as random variables; more advanced models incorporate genotype-by-environment interactions [1]. In the PRS context, methods include shrinkage/regularization techniques such as Lasso and ridge regression, Bayesian approaches with prior distributions, and P-value thresholding with LD clumping [3][4]. (4) **Prediction accuracy** — measured as the correlation between observed and predicted values (or GEBV accuracy) for breeding, or via discriminative metrics like the area under the ROC curve (AUC) for disease risk; accuracy depends on trait heritability, training population size and diversity, marker density, relatedness between training and target individuals",
