of juvenile candidates [1]; and **human genetics**, where polygenic risk scores stratify disease risk for conditions such as coronary artery disease and type 2 diabetes, potentially enabling earlier screening and preventive intervention [3][5].\n\n**Key methodological components.** (1) A **training (reference) population** — individuals with both high-quality phenotypes and genome-wide genotypes, from which the model learns marker–trait relationships; a larger, more representative training population generally yields higher accuracy [1]. (2) A **validation population** — separate or held-out individuals used to test model performance, typically via cross-validation [1]. (3) **Statistical/machine learning models** — common approaches include genomic best linear unbiased prediction (GBLUP) and Bayesian methods that treat marker effects as random variables; more advanced models incorporate genotype-by-environment interactions [1]. In the PRS context, methods include shrinkage/regularization techniques such as Lasso and ridge regression, Bayesian approaches with prior distributions, and P-value thresholding with LD clumping [3][4]. (4) **Prediction accuracy** — measured as the correlation between observed and predicted values (or GEBV accuracy) for breeding, or via discriminative metrics like the area under the ROC curve (AUC) for disease risk; accuracy depends on trait heritability, training population size and diversity, marker density, relatedness between training and target individuals, and ancestry matching [1][3][4][5].\n\n**History/origins.** The foundational work is **Meuwissen, Hayes & Goddard (2001), \"Prediction of total genetic value using genome-wide dense marker maps\"** (*Genetics* 157:1819–1829), which introduced the concept of genomic selection by demonstrating that dense, genome-wide marker maps can be used to predict total genetic value [1][6]. The approach was rapidly adopted — notably in dairy cattle breeding starting around 2009, where it reduced reliance on costly progeny testing and increased rates of genetic gain — and has since spread across crops and other livestock species [1].\n\n### Sources\n[1] Genomic Selection in Plant and Animal Breeding: Principles, Models, and Real-World Applications (CD Genomics): https://www.cd-genomics.com/agri/resource-genomic-selection-plant-animal-breeding.html\n[2] Polygenic Risk Score (PRS) — NHGRI Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms: https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Polygenic-Risk-Score-PRS\n[3] Polygenic risk score: a useful clinical instrument for disease prediction and risk categorization (Yun, *Cardiovasc Prev Pharmacother* 2022): https://www.e-jcpp.org/journal/view.php?number=49\n[4] What is a Polygenic Risk Score? (BYU PRS Knowledge Base): https://prs.byu.edu/about.html\n[5] Calculating Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS) in UK Biobank: A Practical Guide for Epidemiologists (*Frontiers in Genetics* 2022): https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/genetics/articles/10.3389/fgene.2022.818574/full\n[6] Meuwissen",
