Development](species-specific-veterinary-cytokine-panel-development.htm)\n  + [Mucosal Immune Response Assay Development for Animal Vaccines](mucosal-immune-response-assay-development-animal-vaccines.htm)\n  + [Systems Vaccinology Multi-Omics Service for Veterinary Research](systems-vaccinology-multi-omics-service-veterinary-research.htm)\n  + [Veterinary Adjuvant Immunoprofiling Service](veterinary-adjuvant-immunoprofiling-service.htm)\n\n[Company](javascript:)\n\n* [Contact Us](contact)\n* [About Us](about.htm)\n* [Distributors](distributors?c=EU)\n\n\n\n\nKeywords Search\nService Search\nProduct Search\n\n\n\nSearch\n\n\n\n\n* [Home](/)\n* [R&D Solutions](/r-d-solutions.htm)\n* [Animal Breeding and Genetics](/animal-breeding-and-genetics.htm)\n* [Animal Breeding Solutions](/animal-breeding-solutions.htm)\n* [Genomic Selection in Animal Breeding](/genomic-selection-in-animal-breeding.htm)\n\nGenomic Selection in Animal Breeding\n====================================\n\nBioVenic provides genomic selection services to help animal breeding programs predict genomic estimated breeding values (GEBVs), identify superior candidates earlier, and make data-driven selection decisions for economically important species, including cattle, swine, sheep, goats, poultry, and aquaculture species.\n\n Request a Genomic Selection Plan\n\n[Overview](#overview) [Core\nServices](#services) [Workflow](#workflow) [Selection\nStrategy](#platform) [Species\n& Traits](#species-traits) [Published\nEvidence](#published-data) [FAQ](#faq)\n\nOnline Inquiry\n\n\n\nGenomic Selection Services for Faster, Data-Driven Animal Breeding\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nAnimal breeding companies, animal genetics researchers, agricultural biotechnology teams, and R&D institutions increasingly need genomic selection in animal breeding to improve selection accuracy without waiting for long breeding cycles. For cattle, swine, sheep, goats, poultry, aquaculture species, and other economically important animals, conventional breeding value estimation can be limited by costly phenotype collection, environmental noise, late-expressed traits, low-heritability traits, and complex traits controlled by many genomic regions.\n\nBioVenic helps customers convert genotype, phenotype, pedigree, and optional omics data into practical genomic breeding value analysis and GEBV-based decision-making. Through animal genomic selection services covering reference population design, marker QC, model development, validation, candidate ranking, and selection reporting, we support earlier selection, higher breeding accuracy, and more confident breeding program updates.\n\nEarly\n\nCandidate ranking before traits are expressed\n\nAccurate\n\nModel-based GEBV prediction and validation\n\nScalable\n\nSpecies-specific programs and trait panels\n\n Discuss your breeding objective with BioVenic\n\n![Genomic selection service for animal breeding programs](/static/img/2-6-1-Genomic-Selection-in-Animal-Breeding-1.jpg)\n\nGenomic Selection Service Categories\n------------------------------------\n\nAs a service category page, this section highlights BioVenic's three core solutions for\ngenomic selection-driven animal breeding. Each service can be used independently or combined\ninto an integrated decision-support workflow.\n\n### [Genomic Estimated Breeding Value (GEBV) Prediction](/genomic-estimated-breeding-value-gebv-prediction.htm)\n\nPredict genomic breeding values from marker, phenotype, and pedigree data to\nsupport early candidate selection and ranking.\n\n [Explore GEBV Prediction](/genomic-estimated-breeding-value-gebv-prediction.htm)\n\nBioVenic supports genomic estimated breeding value prediction from data readiness review to deployable decision outputs. Project modules can include genotype imputation and marker QC, genomic relationship matrix construction, reference population design, phenotype harmonization, GBLUP, ssGBLUP, Bayesian models, machine learning models, cross-validation, prediction accuracy evaluation, trait-specific model optimization, multi-trait selection index development, and candidate animal scoring. Deliverables can include ranked candidate lists, trait prediction reports, model performance summaries, and recommendations for reference population refinement.\n\n#### Data Preparation\n\nMarker QC, genotype imputation, phenotype alignment, pedigree review, and reference population design.\n\n#### Model Building\n\nGBLUP, ssGBLUP, Bayesian, or machine learning models selected by species, data size, and trait type.\n\n#### Decision Output\n\nGEBV ranking, prediction accuracy evaluation, multi-trait selection index, and breeding recommendations.\n\n### [Multi-omics Selection in Animal Breeding](/multi-omics-selection-in-animal-breeding.htm)\n\nIntegrate genomic data with transcriptomic, metabolomic, epigenomic, or other\nomics layers to capture biological signals beyond marker effects alone.\n\n* • Supports trait biology interpretation and feature prioritization.\n* • Useful for complex traits such as disease resistance, fertility, growth, feed efficiency, and product quality.\n* • Can be paired with GEBV models to improve decision confidence.\n\n [Explore Multi-omics Selection](/multi-omics-selection-in-animal-breeding.htm)\n\n### [Microbiome Selection in Animal Breeding](/microbiome-selection-in-animal-breeding.htm)\n\nIncorporate microbiome profiles into selection strategies for host-associated\ntraits related to health, nutrition, robustness, and production performance.\n\n* • Links host genetics with microbial community variation and trait outcomes.\n* • Relevant for feed efficiency, gut health, disease resilience, and aquaculture performance.\n* • Supports combined host-marker and microbiome-assisted selection models.\n\n [Explore Microbiome Selection](/microbiome-selection-in-animal-breeding.htm)\n\nEnd-to-End Genomic Selection Workflow\n-------------------------------------\n\nA robust genomic selection program begins with clear breeding goals and reliable data\narchitecture. BioVenic works with customers to define target traits, review existing\nphenotype and pedigree records, choose genotyping or sequencing strategies, and build\nreference populations that can support stable model training. Candidate animals can then\nbe scored early using genome-wide markers, helping teams shorten breeding cycles and\nfocus resources on the most promising individuals.\n\nOur workflow can be adapted to new projects, ongoing breeding programs, or retrospective\ndatasets. Depending on your objective, we can provide complete analysis or selected\nmodules such as marker QC, model comparison, GEBV prediction, multi-trait evaluation,\nand report generation.\n\n#### Reference Population\n\nAnimals with high-quality phenotypes and genome-wide markers.\n\n#### Prediction Model\n\nMarker effect estimation, validation, and trait-specific optimization.\n\n#### Candidate Scoring\n\nEarly GEBV prediction for selection and mating decisions.\n\n#### Program Update\n\nModel refresh as new phenotypes and genotypes become available.\n\n![Genomic Selection Protocol in Animal Genetics (BioVenic Original)](/static/img/2-6-1-Genomic-Selection-in-Animal-Breeding-2.jpg)\n\nFig.1 Workflow of Genomic Selection in Animal Breeding\n\nSelection Strategy Comparison for Animal Breeding Programs\n----------------------------------------------------------\n\nGenomic selection extends earlier approaches by using markers distributed across the whole\ngenome rather than relying only on visible phenotypes or a limited number of marker-QTL\nassociations.\n\n| Selection Approach | Main Data Used | Best-Fit Use Case | Limitation Addressed by Genomic Selection |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Phenotypic Selection | Observed trait records | Simple traits that can be measured early and accurately | Slow for late-expressed, low-heritability, or environment-sensitive traits |\n| Marker-Assisted Selection | Selected markers linked to QTLs | Traits influenced by known major loci | Limited marker coverage may miss polygenic effects |\n| Genomic Selection | Genome-wide marker profiles plus phenotype and pedigree data | Complex, polygenic, costly, or hard-to-measure traits | Captures genome-wide signal to support earlier GEBV-based selection |\n\n### Data Inputs We Can Support\n\n#### Genotype Data\n\nSNP array, sequencing-derived markers, imputed genotypes, or filtered marker matrices.\n\n#### Phenotype Data\n\nGrowth, reproduction, health, feed efficiency, product quality, resilience, and custom traits.\n\n#### Pedigree Records\n\nFamily structure, relationship matrices, cohort metadata, and breeding history.\n\n#### Omics Layers\n\nTranscriptome, metabolome, microbiome, epigenomic, or other data for advanced models.\n\n### What You Can Receive\n\n* ✓Project-specific genomic selection strategy and data readiness assessment.\n* ✓Quality-controlled genotype, phenotype, and optional multi-omics analysis outputs.\n* ✓GEBV prediction results, candidate rankings, validation metrics, and model interpretation.\n* ✓Recommendations for reference population expansion, trait panel design, and future model updates.\n\n Start a Data Review\n\nAnimal Species and Trait Types Supported\n----------------------------------------\n\nBioVenic supports genomic selection planning and data analysis for economically\nimportant animal species. Our team can adapt marker density, phenotype design, model\nscope, and reporting format to the genetic architecture of your target trait and the\nmaturity of your breeding program.\n\nProjects may focus on a single trait, multi-trait index, candidate screening pipeline,\nor recurring selection cycle. We also support feasibility discussions for rare species\nor emerging breeding programs with limited reference data.\n\n Ask About Your Species\n\n### Species Coverage\n\nCattle Swine Sheep Goats Poultry Aquaculture Insects Other Species\n\n### Trait Examples\n\nGrowth Rate Feed Efficiency Fertility Disease Resistance Milk Yield Meat Quality Stress Tolerance Survival\n\n### Designed for Operational Breeding Decisions\n\nWe do not treat genomic selection as a standalone report. BioVenic helps connect\nmodel results with your real breeding decisions, including candidate ranking,\nreplacement selection, mating group prioritization, reference population updates,\nand long-term genetic gain planning.\n\nPeer-Reviewed Evidence for Genomic Selection in Animal Breeding\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nPublished genomic selection studies provide practical implementation logic for animal breeding programs that aim to connect phenotypic records, genome-wide markers, genomic estimated breeding value prediction, and candidate animal selection across multiple species.\n\n### How Published Genomic Selection Workflows Inform Service Design\n\nA 2024 open access publication discussing caprine and ovine genomic selection provides one useful species example of how genomic selection can be implemented in animal breeding. In that example, sheep and goat programs use reference populations that combine phenotypic records with genome-wide marker information, and prediction models built from these datasets can estimate genomic breeding values for candidate animals before long-term phenotypes are available.\n\nThis sheep and goat example is not the limit of BioVenic's service scope. The same analytical logic can be adapted for livestock genomic selection, genomic selection for cattle, swine, poultry, aquaculture, and other breeding programs: define the breeding objective, prepare reliable data, train and validate prediction models, apply GEBV scoring to candidates, and update the model as new data accumulates. The approach is especially relevant for complex traits where conventional phenotyping alone can be slow, expensive, or incomplete.\n\n Contact BioVenic to build a genomic selection workflow\n\n![Genomic Selection Technical Route. (OA Literature)](/static/img/genomic-selection-in-animal-breeding.jpg)\n\nFig. 2. Technical route of genomic selection. 3,4\n\nWhy Choose BioVenic for Genomic Selection Analysis\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nBioVenic combines animal breeding knowledge, genetics expertise, and bioinformatics\nworkflows to help turn raw genomic data into actionable breeding decisions.\n\n#### Breeding-Focused Analysis\n\nWe align modeling choices with your trait biology, population structure, selection\nintensity, and operational breeding timeline.\n\n#### Flexible Data Integration\n\nProjects can include SNP array data, sequencing-derived markers, pedigree records,\nphenotypes, environmental metadata, and optional omics layers.\n\n#### Transparent Deliverables\n\nReports can include data QC summaries, model performance metrics, GEBV tables, candidate\nranking groups, and interpretation notes.\n\n#### Long-Term Support\n\nBioVenic can support one-time analysis, recurring selection cycles, model refreshes, and\nreference population improvement plans.\n\nReady to convert genomic data into selection decisions?\n-------------------------------------------------------\n\nShare your species, traits, sample size, and available datasets. BioVenic will help you evaluate\nthe best genomic selection path for your breeding program.\n\n Talk to a Genomics Specialist\n\nFrequently Asked Questions\n--------------------------\n\nWhat data do I need to start a genomic selection project? \n\nA typical project requires genotype data, phenotype records, and basic animal metadata\nsuch as species, breed, age, sex, cohort, farm or environment, and pedigree information\nwhen available. If your project is still at the planning stage, BioVenic can help define\nreference population size, trait recording strategy, marker density, and data quality\nrequirements before analysis begins.\n\nCan BioVenic support projects with limited phenotype records? \n\nYes. We can review the available records and recommend practical options such as trait\nharmonization, cohort filtering, reference population expansion, model scope adjustment,\nor phased implementation. For very small datasets, we may recommend feasibility analysis\nbefore full GEBV prediction.\n\nWhich animal species can be analyzed? \n\nBioVenic supports genomic selection analysis for livestock, poultry, aquaculture\nspecies, insects, and selected project-specific species. Common examples include cattle,\nswine, sheep, goats, chickens, ducks, fish, and other production animals. Feasibility\ndepends on marker resources, phenotype quality, population structure, and project goals.\n\nHow are GEBV prediction results delivered? \n\nDeliverables can include a technical report, genotype and phenotype QC summary, model\ncomparison results, validation metrics, GEBV tables, candidate ranking files, and\ninterpretation notes. We can also tailor report formats to internal breeding decision\nmeetings or recurring selection cycles.\n\nCan genomic selection be combined with multi-omics or microbiome data? \n\nYes. BioVenic provides both multi-omics selection and microbiome selection services.\nThese approaches can add biological context to genomic prediction, support complex trait\ninterpretation, and help identify additional signals associated with health, feed\nefficiency, resilience, and production traits.\n\n#### Reference\n\n1. Ibtisham, Fahar, *et al*. \"Genomic selection and its application in animal breeding.\"\n   The Thai Journal of Veterinary Medicine 47.3 (2017): 301-310. <https://doi.org/10.56808/2985-1130.2838>\n2. Goddard, M. E., and B. J. Hayes. \"Genomic selection.\" Journal of Animal breeding and\n   Genetics 124.6 (2007): 323-330. <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1439-0388.2007.00702.x>\n3. Zhang, Linyun, *et al*. \"Caprine and ovine genomic selection--progress and\n   application.\" Animals 14.18 (2024): 2659. <https://doi.org/10.3390/ani14182659>\n4. Distributed under Open Access license [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/),\n   without modification.\n\n\n\n\n\nGet a quote\n\nWe're excited to learn more about your project and provide you with a customized quote tailored to your needs. Please fill out the form below, and we'll get back to you as soon as possible.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\\* Email\n\nName\n\nPhone\n\nCompany/Institute\n\n\\* Products or Services Interested\n\nMessage\n\nInquiry Now\n\n\n\nThis site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google [Privacy Policy](https://policies.google.com/privacy) and [Terms of Service](https://policies.google.com/terms) apply.\n\n\n\n\n![BioVenic](../static/images/logo-white.svg)   \n  \n\n**USA:**   \n**Phone:**   \n**Email:**\n\n#### Newsletter\n\nSubscribe\n\n* [**About Us**](about.htm)\n* [**Contact Us**](contact)\n* [**Resources**](resources.htm)\n\n#### [Animal Species We Focus](by-species.htm)\n\n* [Swine](swine.htm)\n* [Ruminants](ruminants.htm)\n* [Poultry](poultry.htm)\n* [Aquaculture](aquaculture.htm)\n* [Companion Animals](companion-animals.htm)\n* [Insects](economic-insects.htm)\n\n#### [R&D Solutions](r-d-solutions.htm)\n\n* [Animal Breeding and Genetics](animal-breeding-and-genetics.htm)\n* [Animal Health and Veterinary Biologicals Development](animal-health-veterinary-biologicals-development.htm)\n* [Animal Nutrition and Feed Development](animal-nutrition-and-feed-development.htm)\n\n#### [Products](category-products-1.htm)\n\n* Animal-derived Products\n* [Veterinary Biologicals Development](category-veterinary-biologicals-development-3.htm)\n\nCopyright ©   BioVenic. All rights reserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInquiry Basket\n\n---\n\n## Minimum marker densities for accurate genomic predictions and heritability estimates in three major North American and European spruce species | Heredity\n**URL:** https://www.nature.com/articles/s41437-026-00836-7\n\nMinimum marker densities for accurate genomic predictions and heritability estimates in three major North American and European spruce species | Heredity\n\n\n\n[Skip to main content](#content)\n\nThank you for visiting nature.com. You are using a browser version with limited support for CSS. To obtain\nthe best experience, we recommend you use a more up to date browser (or turn off compatibility mode in\nInternet Explorer). In the meantime, to ensure continued support, we are displaying the site without styles\nand JavaScript.\n\nAdvertisement\n\n[![Advertisement](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/ad?iu=/285/hdy.nature.com/article&sz=728x90&c=711389115&t=pos%3Dtop%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41437-026-00836-7%26doi%3D10.1038/s41437-026-00836-7%26techmeta%3D23,45%26subjmeta%3D1145,158,208,212,2464,631,704,748%26kwrd%3DComparative+genomics,Ecological+genetics,Forestry)](//pubads.g.doubleclick.net/gampad/jump?iu=/285/hdy.nature.com/article&sz=728x90&c=711389115&t=pos%3Dtop%26type%3Darticle%26artid%3Ds41437-026-00836-7%26doi%3D10.1038/s41437-026-00836-7%26techmeta%3D23,45%26subjmeta%3D1145,158,208,212,2464,631,704,748%26kwrd%3DComparative+genomics,Ecological+genetics,Forestry)\n\n\n\n[![Heredity](https://media.springernature.com/full/nature-cms/uploads/product/hdy/header-3c785d60c600344908a3062a4389be3b.svg)](/hdy)\n\n* [View all journals](https://www.nature.com/siteindex)\n* [Saved research](/saved-research)\n* [Search](#search-menu)\n* [Log in](https://idp.nature.com/auth/personal/springernature?redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41437-026-00836-7)\n\n* [Content\n  Explore content](#explore)\n* [About the journal](#about-the-journal)\n* [Publish with us](#publish-with-us)\n\n* [Sign up for alerts](https://journal-alerts.springernature.com/subscribe?journal_id=41437)\n* [RSS feed](https://www.nature.com/hdy.rss)\n\n1. [nature](/)\n2. [heredity](/hdy)\n3. [articles](/hdy/articles?type=article)\n4. article\n\nMinimum marker densities for accurate genomic predictions and heritability estimates in three major North American and European spruce species\n\n[Download PDF](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7.pdf)\n\n[Download PDF](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7.pdf)\n\n* Article\n* [Open access](https://www.springernature.com/gp/open-science/about/the-fundamentals-of-open-access-and-open-research)\n* Published: 08 May 2026\n\nMinimum marker densities for accurate genomic predictions and heritability estimates in three major North American and European spruce species\n==============================================================================================================================================\n\n* [André Soro](#auth-Andr_-Soro-Aff1-Aff2) \n  [ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-1694-6234](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1694-6234)[1](#Aff1),[2](#Aff2),\n* [Patrick Lenz](#auth-Patrick-Lenz-Aff1-Aff2)[1](#Aff1),[2](#Aff2),\n* [Jean Beaulieu](#auth-Jean-Beaulieu-Aff1) \n  [ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-5806-7353](https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5806-7353)[1](#Aff1),\n* [Jean-Philippe Laverdière](#auth-Jean_Philippe-Laverdi_re-Aff2-Aff3)[2](#Aff2),[3](#Aff3),\n* [Simon Nadeau](#auth-Simon-Nadeau-Aff2)[2](#Aff2),\n* [France Gagnon](#auth-France-Gagnon-Aff1)[1](#Aff1),\n* [Funda Ogut](#auth-Funda-Ogut-Aff3)[3](#Aff3),\n* [Harry X. Wu](#auth-Harry_X_-Wu-Aff4) \n  [ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-7072-4704](https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7072-4704)[4](#Aff4),\n* [Martin Perron](#auth-Martin-Perron-Aff1-Aff5)[1](#Aff1),[5](#Aff5) &\n* …\n* [Jean Bousquet](#auth-Jean-Bousquet-Aff1)[1](#Aff1)\n\nShow authors\n\n[*Heredity*](/hdy)\n**volume 135**, pages 342–357 (2026) [Cite this article](#citeas)\n\n[Save article](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/save-research?_csrf=E8CwwVHJRcAlhSLxfuengxJhJSGA3-o3)\n\n[View saved research](/saved-research)\n\n* 4295 Accesses\n* 1 Citations\n* [Metrics details](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/metrics)\n\nAbstract\n--------\n\nGenomic selection (GS) is being increasingly used in tree breeding with the aim to accelerate genetic gains by shortening the long breeding cycles. However, high genotyping costs remain a challenge. This study aimed to determine the optimal marker density in genome coverage to maximize GS accuracy and precision of heritability estimates for growth and wood quality traits. Thousands of SNPs representative of the exome of three major spruce species were used: 18,275 SNPs for black spruce (representing 10,894 distinct gene loci), 11,328 SNPs for white spruce (8647 gene loci), and 116,765 SNPs for Norway spruce (20,695 gene loci). For each species, a similar experimental design was used with related full-sib families replicated on two sites, and GBLUP prediction models were developed. The effect of varying the number of SNPs was examined by resampling subsets from 500 to 100,000 SNPs. Results indicated that plateaus in heritability estimates were reached as the marker density increased, stabilizing between 4000 and 8000 SNPs for a spruce genome size of around 2000 centimorgans, a trend consistent across all traits and species. Predictive ability and prediction accuracy both increased with the number of SNPs up to a similar level, beyond which further improvements were marginal. Such minimum marker densities should be financially affordable for most spruce breeding programs, striking a balance between the need for maximizing GS accuracy and that for minimizing genotyping costs. These findings should support the further deployment of GS in conifer breeding programs, with high selection precision and by reducing the financial burden of very high-density SNP coverage, even for conifers characterized by large giga-genomes.\n\n### Similar content being viewed by others\n\n![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-025-00747-z/MediaObjects/41437_2025_747_Fig1_HTML.png)\n\n### [Revealing stable SNPs and genomic prediction insights across environments enhance breeding strategies of productivity, defense, and climate-adaptability traits in white spruce](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41437-025-00747-z?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticle\nOpen access\n12 February 2025\n\n![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-022-00508-2/MediaObjects/41437_2022_508_Fig1_HTML.png)\n\n### [Improving lodgepole pine genomic evaluation using spatial correlation structure and SNP selection with single-step GBLUP](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41437-022-00508-2?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticle\n18 February 2022\n\n![](https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41598-024-53462-w/MediaObjects/41598_2024_53462_Fig1_HTML.png)\n\n### [Genomic prediction for agronomic traits in a diverse Flax (*Linum usitatissimum* L.) germplasm collection](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53462-w?fromPaywallRec=false)\n\nArticle\nOpen access\n08 February 2024\n\n### Explore related subjects\n\nDiscover the latest articles and news in related subjects.\n\n* [Comparative genomics](/subjects/comparative-genomics)\n* [Ecological genetics](/subjects/ecological-genetics)\n* [Forestry](/subjects/forestry)\n\nIntroduction\n------------\n\nGenomic selection (GS) is a predictive method that relies on dense genome-wide marker coverage to model the full spectrum of quantitative trait loci (QTL) effects across the genome (Meuwissen et al. [2001](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR60 \"Meuwissen THE, Hayes BJ, Goddard ME (2001) Prediction of total genetic value using genome-wide dense marker maps. Genetics 157:1819–1829\")). But it also depends on the relatedness of individuals making up the training and target populations (Habier et al. [2007](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR40 \"Habier D, Fernando RL, Dekkers JCM (2007) The impact of genetic relationship information on genome-assisted breeding values. Genet Soc Am 177:2389–2397\"); Zapata-Valenzuela et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR98 \"Zapata-Valenzuela J, Isik F, Maltecca C, Wegrzyn J, Neale D, McKeand S et al. (2012) SNP markers trace familial linkages in a cloned population of Pinus taeda—prospects for genomic selection. Tree Genet Genomes 8:1307–1318\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")). It has been applied in numerous animal (Meuwissen et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR59 \"Meuwissen T, Hayes B, Goddard M (2016) Genomic selection: a paradigm shift in animal breeding. Anim Front 6:6–14\")) and plant breeding programs (Crossa et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR23 \"Crossa J, Pérez-Rodríguez P, Cuevas J, Montesinos-López O, Jarquín D, de los Campos G et al. (2017) Genomic selection in plant breeding: methods, models, and perspectives. Trends Plant Sci 22:961–975\")), and is being implemented progressively more into tree breeding programs (Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\"); Beaulieu et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\"), [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\"); Grattapaglia et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR37 \"Grattapaglia D (2022) Twelve years into genomic selection in forest trees: climbing the slope of enlightenment of marker assisted tree breeding. Forests 13(25p):10\")).\n\nConventional breeding methods applied to forest trees, usually characterized by lengthy breeding cycles and great efforts, particularly for conifers (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")), have often struggled to keep pace with the urgent need for improved tree varieties that can adapt to rapidly changing market and environmental conditions, especially with the intensification of climate change effects (Depardieu et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR26 \"Depardieu C, Girardin MP, Nadeau S, Lenz P, Bousquet J, Isabel N (2020) Adaptive genetic variation to drought in a widely distributed conifer suggests a potential for increasing forest resilience in a drying climate. N Phytol 227:427–439\"); Benomar et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR9 \"Benomar L, Bousquet J, Perron M, Beaulieu J, Lamara M (2022) Tree maladaptation under mid-latitude early spring warming and late cold spell: implications for assisted migration. Front Plant Sci 13(12p):920852\"); Laverdière et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR48 \"Laverdière J-P, Lenz P, Nadeau S, Depardieu C, Isabel N, Perron M et al. (2022) Breeding for adaptation to climate change: genomic selection for drought response in a white spruce multi-site polycross test. Evol Appl 15:383–402\")). GS has the potential to help boost genetic gain per time unit by reducing the need for lengthy field evaluation of phenotypes as in conventional breeding programs (Grattapaglia and Resende [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR6 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014a) Accuracy of genomic selection models in a large population of open-pollinated families in white spruce. Heredity 113:343–352\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"), [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"), [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR51 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Azaiez A, Gérardi S, Deslauriers M, Perron M et al. (2020b) Genomic prediction for hastening and improving efficiency of forward selection in conifer polycross mating designs: an example from white spruce. Heredity 124:562–578\"); Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")), while increasing selection intensity by screening thousands of candidates and facilitating multi-trait selection addressing multiple breeding objectives (Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"); Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\")). GS offers consequently significant advantages over conventional breeding strategies, which are often time-consuming and costly, particularly for long-lived species such as spruces (Beaulieu et al. [2014a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR6 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014a) Accuracy of genomic selection models in a large population of open-pollinated families in white spruce. Heredity 113:343–352\"); Park et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR69 \"Park Y-S, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Multi-varietal forestry integrating genomic selection and somatic embryogenesis. In: Park Y-S, Bonga J, Moon HK (eds) Vegetative propagation of forest trees. National Institute of Forest Science, Seoul, South Korea, pp 302–322\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"), [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR51 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Azaiez A, Gérardi S, Deslauriers M, Perron M et al. (2020b) Genomic prediction for hastening and improving efficiency of forward selection in conifer polycross mating designs: an example from white spruce. Heredity 124:562–578\"), Lebedev et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR49 \"Lebedev VG, Lebedeva TN, Chernodubov AI, Shestibratov KA (2020) Genomic selection for forest tree improvement: methods, achievements and perspectives. Forests 11(36p):11\"); Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\")). GS is thus positioned as a crucial tool to help tree breeders meet the rising demand for high-quality wood fibers for several uses worldwide, but also faces the growing challenges imposed by rapid climate and environmental changes to more rapidly select and produce adapted seedling stocks (Park et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR69 \"Park Y-S, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Multi-varietal forestry integrating genomic selection and somatic embryogenesis. In: Park Y-S, Bonga J, Moon HK (eds) Vegetative propagation of forest trees. National Institute of Forest Science, Seoul, South Korea, pp 302–322\"), Beaulieu et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR8 \"Beaulieu J, Nadeau S, Ding C, Celedon JM, Azaiez A, Ritland C et al. (2020) Genomic selection for resistance to spruce budworm in white spruce and relationships with growth and wood quality traits. Evol Appl 13:2704–2722\"), Laverdière et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR48 \"Laverdière J-P, Lenz P, Nadeau S, Depardieu C, Isabel N, Perron M et al. (2022) Breeding for adaptation to climate change: genomic selection for drought response in a white spruce multi-site polycross test. Evol Appl 15:383–402\")).\n\nGS leverages the vast amount of genetic information made available through high-throughput genotyping technologies to make accurate predictions of individuals’ genetic potential for valued quantitative traits (Meuwissen et al. [2001](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR60 \"Meuwissen THE, Hayes BJ, Goddard ME (2001) Prediction of total genetic value using genome-wide dense marker maps. Genetics 157:1819–1829\")). This is even more so when predictions are estimated for untested crosses in a tree breeding context, as genomic markers can better trace non-additive effects such as dominance (Nadeau et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). Growth traits, which are widely used in tree breeding strategies, are generally under lower additive genetic control and often carry important dominance components, whereas wood quality traits are mostly under moderate to strong additive genetic control (Lenz et al. 2020). Thus, GS has the potential to be efficient in selecting for traits of various genetic architectures (Grattapaglia and Resende [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) and to accelerate the identification of superior individuals for reforestation based on their growth, wood quality, and resistance to biotic or abiotic stressors (Beaulieu et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR8 \"Beaulieu J, Nadeau S, Ding C, Celedon JM, Azaiez A, Ritland C et al. (2020) Genomic selection for resistance to spruce budworm in white spruce and relationships with growth and wood quality traits. Evol Appl 13:2704–2722\"); Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"); Laverdière et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR48 \"Laverdière J-P, Lenz P, Nadeau S, Depardieu C, Isabel N, Perron M et al. (2022) Breeding for adaptation to climate change: genomic selection for drought response in a white spruce multi-site polycross test. Evol Appl 15:383–402\")). Most economically important traits are of a quantitative nature and controlled by many genes, each having a small effect on the phenotype (White et al. [2007](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR95 \"White TL, Adams WT, Neale DB (2007) Forest genetics. Cabi Publishing, Wallingford\")). Therefore, for GS to be successful, it is essential that an adequate number of markers is used to achieve a sufficient genome coverage and that at least one of these markers is in more or less complete linkage disequilibrium (LD) with each of the QTLs that affect the quantitative trait of interest (Meuwissen et al. [2001](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR60 \"Meuwissen THE, Hayes BJ, Goddard ME (2001) Prediction of total genetic value using genome-wide dense marker maps. Genetics 157:1819–1829\"); Hayes and Goddard [2010](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR44 \"Hayes B, Goddard M (2010) Genome-wide association and genomic selection in animal breeding. Genome 53:876–883\")).\n\nEarly GS applications in forest trees were bolstered by advancements in genotyping technologies, statistics, and information technology (IT). High-throughput genotyping technologies were indeed made available early to forest geneticists (e.g., Pavy et al. [2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR72 \"Pavy N, Pelgas B, Beauseigle S, Blais S, Gagnon F, Gosselin I et al. (2008) Enhancing genetic mapping of complex genomes through the design of highly-multiplexed SNP arrays: application to the large and unsequenced genomes of white spruce and black spruce. BMC Genomics 9(17p):21\"), [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR74 \"Pavy N, Gagnon F, Rigault P, Blais S, Deschênes A, Boyle B et al. (2013) Development of high-density SNP genotyping arrays for white spruce (Picea glauca) and transferability to subtropical and nordic congeners. Mol Ecol Resour 13:324–336\")). Various statistical methods, such as ridge regression, Best Linear Unbiased Prediction (BLUP), especially applied to genomic data (GBLUP), as well as Bayesian methods, allowed for more accurate genetic predictions by effectively incorporating dense marker information and accounting for the complex genetic architecture of quantitative traits (De Los Campos et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR17 \"De Los Campos G, Hickey JM, Pong-Wong R, Daetwyler HD, Calus MPL (2013) Whole-genome regression and prediction methods applied to plant and animal breeding. Genetics 193:327–345\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR6 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014a) Accuracy of genomic selection models in a large population of open-pollinated families in white spruce. Heredity 113:343–352\")). Algorithms and softwares were rapidly developed by statisticians under the R Project (R Core Team [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR80 \"R. Core Team (2023) R: a language and environment for statistical computing\")) and made available to the genetics community. Grattapaglia and Resende ([2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) demonstrated the potential of GS to improve traits with moderate to high heritability in *Eucalyptus* and, through simulations, set the baseline for similar approaches in other broadleaf and conifer species.\n\nIn recent years, significant progress has been made in the publication of proof of concept studies of GS in conifers, especially for species with advanced genetic improvement program underway (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")), such as black spruce (*Picea mariana* [Mill.] B.S.P.) (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")), white spruce (*Picea glauca* [Moench] Voss) (Beaulieu et al. [2014a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR6 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014a) Accuracy of genomic selection models in a large population of open-pollinated families in white spruce. Heredity 113:343–352\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"), [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR8 \"Beaulieu J, Nadeau S, Ding C, Celedon JM, Azaiez A, Ritland C et al. (2020) Genomic selection for resistance to spruce budworm in white spruce and relationships with growth and wood quality traits. Evol Appl 13:2704–2722\"); Ratcliffe et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR81 \"Ratcliffe B, El-Dien OG, Cappa EP, Porth I, Klápště J, Chen C et al. (2017) Single-step BLUP with varying genotyping effort in open-pollinated Picea glauca. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 7:935–942\"); Laverdière et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR48 \"Laverdière J-P, Lenz P, Nadeau S, Depardieu C, Isabel N, Perron M et al. (2022) Breeding for adaptation to climate change: genomic selection for drought response in a white spruce multi-site polycross test. Evol Appl 15:383–402\"); Nadeau et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")), Norway spruce (*Picea abies* [L.] Karst.) (Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\"), [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR22 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Westin J, Rosario M, Gil G et al. (2019) Increased prediction ability in Norway spruce trials using a marker x environment interaction and non-additive genomic selection model. J Hered 110:830–843\"); Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\")), loblolly pine (*Pinus taeda* L.) (Resende et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR82 \"Resende JFR, Muñoz P, Resende MDV, Garrick DJ, Fernando RL, Davis JM et al. (2012) Accuracy of genomic selection methods in a standard data set of loblolly pine (Pinus taeda L.). Genetics 190:1503–1510\"); Zapata-Valenzuela et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR97 \"Zapata-Valenzuela J, Whetten RW, Neale D, McKeand S, Isik F (2013) Genomic estimated breeding values using genomic relationship matrices in a cloned population of loblolly pine. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 3:909–916\")), maritime pine (*Pinus pinaster* Aiton) (Bartholomé et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR3 \"Bartholomé J, Van Heerwaarden J, Isik F, Boury C, Vidal M, Plomion C et al. (2016) Performance of genomic prediction within and across generations in maritime pine. BMC Genomics 17(14p):604\"), Monterey pine (*Pinus radiata* D. Don) (Li and Dungey [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR54 \"Li Y, Dungey HS (2018) Expected benefit of genomic selection over forward selection in conifer breeding and deployment. PLoS ONE 13(21p):0208232\"); Freeman et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR29 \"Freeman JS, Slavov GT, Butler JB, Frickey T, Graham NJ, Klápště J et al. (2022) High density linkage maps, genetic architecture, and genomic prediction of growth and wood properties in Pinus radiata. BMC Genomics 23(16p):731\")), lodgepole pine (*Pinus contorta* Douglas) (Ukrainetz and Mansfield [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR90 \"Ukrainetz NK, Mansfield SD (2020) Assessing the sensitivities of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in lodgepole pine using Bayesian models. Tree Genet Genomes 16(19p):14\")), Douglas-Fir (*Pseudotsuga menziesii* Mirb. [Franco]) (Thistlethwaite et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR88 \"Thistlethwaite FR, Ratcliffe B, Klápště J, Porth I, Chen C, Stoehr MU et al. (2017) Genomic prediction accuracies in space and time for height and wood density of Douglas-fir using exome capture as the genotyping platform. BMC Genomics 18(16p):930\")), Japanese cedar (*Cryptomeria japonica* D. Don) (Hiraoka et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR46 \"Hiraoka Y, Fukatsu E, Mishima K, Hirao T, Teshima KM, Tamura M et al. (2018) Potential of genome-wide studies in unrelated plus trees of a coniferous species, Cryptomeria japonica (Japanese cedar). Front Plant Sci 9(15p):1322\")), western red cedar (*Thuja plicata* Donn ex Don) (Gamal El-Dien et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR31 \"Gamal, El-Dien O, Shalev TJ, Yuen MMS, Stirling R, Daniels LD, Breinholt JW et al. (2022) Genomic selection reveals hidden relatedness and increased breeding efficiency in western redcedar polycross breeding. Evol Appl 15:1291–1312\"), [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR32 \"Gamal El-Dien O, Shalev TJ, Yuen MMS, Van der Merwe L, Kirst M, Yanchuk AD et al. (2024) Genomic selection in western redcedar: from proof of concept to operational application. N Phytol 244:588–602\")). These studies relied on SNP markers to capture the genetic variation within species, facilitating the accurate prediction of complex traits such as growth rate, wood quality, and pest resistance or tolerance.\n\nDespite these technical progress, several challenges remain in the implementation of GS in forest tree breeding. One of the primary issues is to increase marker density to improve capture of short-range LD, whilst determining the optimal number of markers required for accurate genomic predictions. For across-generation GS accuracy in advanced tree breeding programs, increasing marker density might even be more important to capture short LD. While high-density SNP arrays might provide more precise genetic information, they are often cost-prohibitive for large-scale tree breeding programs. However, research by Habier et al. ([2007](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR40 \"Habier D, Fernando RL, Dekkers JCM (2007) The impact of genetic relationship information on genome-assisted breeding values. Genet Soc Am 177:2389–2397\")) highlighted the trade-offs between marker density, cost, and prediction accuracy, emphasizing the need for cost-effective strategies that do not compromise the reliability of genetic predictions.\n\nSimulations demonstrated early on that both marker density and breeding population status number (or effective population size) have important impacts on the accuracy of GS (Grattapaglia and Resende [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")). Using a meta-analysis of GS studies in tree species, Beaulieu et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")) confirmed that the higher the breeding population status number, the lower the GS accuracy for a marker density of similar magnitude. They also showed that, in the range of numbers of markers used in published studies of GS in forest tree species, there was no significant variation in GS model accuracy. In contrast to tree breeding, genomic selection (GS) is more advanced in plant and animal breeding (Schaeffer [2006](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR84 \"Schaeffer LR (2006) Strategy for applying genome-wide selection in dairy cattle. J Anim Breed Genet 123:218–223\"); Hayes et al. [2009](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR45 \"Hayes BJ, Bowman PJ, Chamberlain AJ, Goddard ME (2009) Invited review: Genomic selection in dairy cattle: progress and challenges. J Dairy Sci 92:433–443\")). In these fields, several studies investigated the effect of marker density on prediction accuracy, including in wheat (Norman et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR65 \"Norman A, Taylor J, Edwards J, Kuchel H (2018) Optimising genomic selection in wheat: effect of marker density, population size and population structure on prediction accuracy. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 8:2889–2899\")), maize (Guo et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR39 \"Guo R, Dhliwayo T, Mageto EK, Palacios-Rojas N, Lee M, Yu D et al. (2020) Genomic prediction of kernel zinc concentration in multiple maize populations using genotyping-by-sequencing and repeat amplification sequencing markers. Front Plant Sci 11:534\")), alfalfa (Wang et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR94 \"Wang H, Bai Y, Biligetu B (2024) Effects of SNP marker density and training population size on prediction accuracy in alfalfa (Medicago sativa L.) genomic selection. Plant Genome 17(19p):e20431\")), cattle and pig (Su et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR86 \"Su G, Brøndum RF, Ma P, Guldbrandtsen B, Aamand GP, Lund MS (2012) Comparison of genomic predictions using medium-density (∼54,000) and high-density (∼777,000) single nucleotide polymorphism marker panels in Nordic Holstein and Red Dairy cattle populations. J Dairy Sci 95:4657–4665\"); Zhang et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR99 \"Zhang H, Yin L, Wang M, Yuan X, Liu X (2019) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for agricultural economic traits in maize, cattle, and pig populations. Front Genet 10(10p):189\"); Reverter et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR83 \"Reverter A, Hudson NJ, McWilliam S, Alexandre PA, Li Y, Barlow R et al. (2020) A low-density SNP genotyping panel for the accurate prediction of cattle breeds. J Anim Sci 98(10p):11\")). For instance, Wang et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR94 \"Wang H, Bai Y, Biligetu B (2024) Effects of SNP marker density and training population size on prediction accuracy in alfalfa (Medicago sativa L.) genomic selection. Plant Genome 17(19p):e20431\")) showed in alfalfa that GS prediction accuracy reached a plateau with 3000 SNPs, and with around 5000 SNPs for wheat and maize (Norman et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR65 \"Norman A, Taylor J, Edwards J, Kuchel H (2018) Optimising genomic selection in wheat: effect of marker density, population size and population structure on prediction accuracy. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 8:2889–2899\"); Guo et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR39 \"Guo R, Dhliwayo T, Mageto EK, Palacios-Rojas N, Lee M, Yu D et al. (2020) Genomic prediction of kernel zinc concentration in multiple maize populations using genotyping-by-sequencing and repeat amplification sequencing markers. Front Plant Sci 11:534\")), despite their large differences in their physical genome size.\n\nFor tree species, several authors also reported that both heritability and prediction accuracy estimated by GS appear to level out at quite low marker density (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"); Tan et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR87 \"Tan B, Grattapaglia D, Martins GS, Ferreira KZ, Sundberg B, Ingvarsson PK (2017) Evaluating the accuracy of genomic prediction of growth and wood traits in two Eucalyptus species and their F1 hybrids. BMC Plant Biol 17(15p):110\"); Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\"); Calleja-Rodriguez et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR16 \"Calleja-Rodriguez A, Pan J, Funda T, Chen Z, Baison J, Isik F et al. (2020) Evaluation of the efficiency of genomic versus pedigree predictions for growth and wood quality traits in Scots pine. BMC Genomics 21(17p):796\"); Estopa et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR28 \"Estopa RA, Paludeto JGZ, Müller BSF, de Oliveira RA, Azevedo CF, de Resende MDV et al. (2023) Genomic prediction of growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii using different genomic models and variable SNP genotyping density. New For 54:343–362\")). Hence, it has been hypothesized that only a relatively modest number of markers is needed to capture genetic relatedness in structured breeding populations because prediction accuracy does not appear to be largely controlled by marker-QTL short LD but mostly by relatedness in such populations (Habier et al. [2007](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR40 \"Habier D, Fernando RL, Dekkers JCM (2007) The impact of genetic relationship information on genome-assisted breeding values. Genet Soc Am 177:2389–2397\"); Zapata-Valenzuela et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR98 \"Zapata-Valenzuela J, Isik F, Maltecca C, Wegrzyn J, Neale D, McKeand S et al. (2012) SNP markers trace familial linkages in a cloned population of Pinus taeda—prospects for genomic selection. Tree Genet Genomes 8:1307–1318\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"); Grattapaglia et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR37 \"Grattapaglia D (2022) Twelve years into genomic selection in forest trees: climbing the slope of enlightenment of marker assisted tree breeding. Forests 13(25p):10\")). However, previous studies have not consistently established the minimum marker density required to reach most accurate GS predictions and precise estimation of genetic parameters, though the level of marker density necessary for this appears to be quite coherent across published studies on tree species (Beaulieu et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")), and in agreement with early simulation results (Grattapaglia and Resende [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) indicating that relatively low marker densities should be sufficient to attain high GS accuracy.\n\nThe various proof of concept studies on the application of GS in spruce species, during the last decade, have relied on a quite variable number of SNPs ranging from about 5000 to 100,000, with most of them being at the lower end of this range (see Beaulieu et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")) for an exhaustive list). For example, Nadeau et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")) used 4092 SNPs from distinct gene loci in their GS study on white spruce, whereas Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) used 116,765 SNPs with quite high redundancy of SNPs per gene locus for their application of GS in Norway spruce. Further tests on the effect of the number of markers used in GS on the heritability estimates obtained and on the prediction accuracy would allow deepening knowledge and contribute to the application of GS in tree breeding programs.\n\nIn the present study, data from three major spruce species in the northern hemisphere were assembled. Black spruce is one of the most widely distributed and resilient conifers in North America’s boreal forests. It is a species of great importance in Canada and the northern United States, particularly for the production of high-quality pulp and solid wood products, especially framing materials (Zhang and Koubaa [2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR100 \"Zhang SY, Koubaa A (2008) Softwoods of Eastern Canada: their silvics, characteristics, manufacturing and end-uses. FPInnovations, Forintek Division, Quebec City, Canada\")). As for many other commercially valuable tree species, it is a key focus for breeding programs aimed at improving various growth and wood quality traits (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")), and is widely planted in Ontario and Quebec (Natural Resources Canada [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR63 \"Natural Resources Canada (2024) The State of Canada’s Forests: annual report 2024. Natural Resources Canada, Ottawa, Canada, p 154\")).\n\nOf a similar geographic range, white spruce is another commercially significant tree species in the boreal forests of North America. It is reproductively isolated and quite phylogenetically remote from black spruce (Bouillé et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR14 \"Bouillé M, Senneville S, Bousquet J (2011) Discordant mtDNA and cpDNA phylogenies indicate geographic speciation and reticulation as driving factors for the diversification of the genus Picea. Tree Genet Genomes 7(3):469–484\")), is also widely utilised in the production of pulpwood and solid wood products (Zhang and Koubaa [2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR100 \"Zhang SY, Koubaa A (2008) Softwoods of Eastern Canada: their silvics, characteristics, manufacturing and end-uses. FPInnovations, Forintek Division, Quebec City, Canada\")), and the subject of many breeding programs (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")). Given to its high survival rate, adaptability to diverse ecological conditions, and rapid growth, it is one of the most productive conifers in boreal and temperate forests of Canada and is commonly planted, particularly in Alberta, Quebec, and the Maritime provinces.\n\nNorway spruce is native to Scandinavia, Eastern Europe, and the mountainous regions of Western and Central Europe, and it represents the most important conifer for lumber and pulp and paper production in Europe (Hannrup et al. [2004](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR43 \"Hannrup B, Cahalan C, Chantre G, Grabner M, Karlsson B, Le Bayon I et al. (2004) Genetic parameters of growth and wood quality traits in Picea abies. Scand J Res 19:14–29\"); Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")). Indeed, it contributes to 40% of the total growth in European nordic forests, establishing it as a highly important commercial tree species and the subject of many tree breeding programs (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\")). It has also been introduced in eastern Canada, where it is one of the most productive conifers in plantations (Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\")). Breeding programs have hence been developed for this species to enhance various traits, including climate change adaptation, wood quality, and eastern white pine weevil resistance. Altogether, the economic and ecological importance of these three species justify the quest for more efficient breeding methods and make them good candidates for the operationalization of GS in tree breeding efforts.\n\nBased on extensive datasets drawn from operational breeding trials for these three widely reforested spruce species of the northern hemisphere, the present study aims to determine the optimal marker density that maximizes GS prediction accuracy and the precision of genetic parameter estimates for traits with contrasted heritability in spruce breeding populations. Using the same GS modeling approach for the three species, our objective was to determine at which level of marker density, estimates of heritability and GS prediction accuracy for both growth and wood quality traits reached plateaus of values. In short, these plateaus were attained with less than 10,000 markers, beyond which further improvements were marginal and not significant.\n\nMaterials and methods\n---------------------\n\n### Sampling of plant material\n\nFor this study, three major datasets, respectively, for black, white, and Norway spruces, were used. The black spruce dataset was collected in a 33-year-old progeny trial previously used in a previous GS study (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")) and replicated on two sites, Matapedia (48° 32’N, 67° 25’W, altitude: 216 m) and Robidoux (latitude: 48° 18’N, longitude: 65° 31’W, altitude: 275 m). The 918 sampled individuals belonged to 34 controlled-pollinated families derived from a partial diallel mating design. The 46 parents originated from 9 provenances from the Canadian provinces of Ontario, New Brunswick, and Manitoba, and one from Maine in the northeastern United States. Progeny were raised as rooted cuttings in a nursery for 3 years and then established in 1991 on two forest sites using a randomized complete block design, with 4-tree row plots and a 2 m by 2 m spacing. The test contained 126 clonal replicates with two to five ramets per clone, but only one ramet per clone was randomly chosen for analysis.\n\nFor the white spruce dataset, 988 18-year-old trees were sampled from 56 full-sib families originating from 39 parents arranged in a partial diallel, corresponding to a large subset of the trees analysed by Nadeau et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). In this study, we used material from two of the six breeding groups, namely the breeding groups 3 and 4 as labelled in Nadeau et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). The progeny test was established on two contrasting sites, Asselin (47° 55’ N, 68° 26’ W, altitude: 278 m) and St. Casimir (46° 42’ N,72° 06’ W, altitude: 52 m). The experimental layout followed a randomized complete block design with 10 replications. Trees were planted in row plots, with each plot consisting of five trees spaced 2 m by 2 m apart.\n\nFor Norway spruce, a dataset previously published by Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) was used. Briefly, 1370 individuals were selected from two 28-year-old control-pollinated progeny trials replicating the same 128 full-sib families from a partial diallel mating design that consisted of 55 parents originating from Northern Sweden. Five to 20 trees per family per site were selected from the Vindeln (64.30°N, 19.67°E, altitude: 325 m) and Hädanberg (63.58°N, 18.19°E, altitude: 240 m) testing sites of the same age. A completely randomized design without a designed pre-block was used in the Vindeln trial (site 1), which was divided into 44 post-blocks. The same design was also used in the Hädanberg trial (site 2) with 44 post-blocks, but also an extra design with 47 extra plots. After a spatial analysis, the 47 plots were combined into two large post-blocks for final analyses (Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")).\n\n### Trait phenotyping\n\nA similar set of phenotypic traits was considered for all three species. For black spruce, three phenotypic traits were assessed at 30 years after plantation: tree height, diameter at breast height (DBH, measured at 1.3 meters above ground), and wood density. In 2021, a wood increment core was extracted from the south-facing side of each tree. The cores were stored in a freezer, conditioned to 7% moisture content, and cut to a thickness of 1.68 mm prior to X-ray densitometry analysis using Quintek Measurement Systems (Knoxville, TN). Wood density was calculated as a ring area-weighted mean from the recorded pith-to-bark wood density profiles.\n\nFor white spruce, four phenotypic traits were considered: tree height, DBH, wood density, and acoustic velocity. They were assessed at 13 years after plantation. Wood density was determined with X-ray densitometry as described above (Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\")). Acoustic velocity, which is a proxy for wood stiffness (Lenz et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR50 \"Lenz P, Auty D, Achim A, Beaulieu J, Mackay J (2013) Genetic improvement of white spruce mechanical wood traits early screening by means of acoustic velocity. Forests 4:575–594\")), was measured on standing trees with the Hitman ST300 tool (Fibre-gen, Christchurch, New Zealand).\n\nFor Norway spruce, three phenotypic traits were considered: tree height at the age of 17 years, Pilodyn pin penetration as a surrogate for wood density, and acoustic velocity using the Hitman ST300 tool.\n\n### SNP genotyping\n\nFor black spruce, 918 trees were genotyped with the new Infinium iSelect SNP array Pmr25k (Illumina, San Diego, CA) (see Gérardi et al. [2025](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR33 \"Gérardi S, Gagnon F, Laroche J, Boyle B, Pavy N, Nadeau S, Soro A et al. (2025) Development and assessment of an exome-wide SNP genotyping array for GWAS, genomic prediction and assessment of introgressive hybridization between black and red spruces, and transferability to white and Norway spruces. bioRxiv \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.14.688480\n                  \n                \") for details), from which 18,275 SNPs segregated in black spruce, representing 12,438 distinct gene loci. For white spruce, two genotyping datasets were combined for same trees: earlier published genotyping data from the Infinium SNP array PgLM3+ (Pavy et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR74 \"Pavy N, Gagnon F, Rigault P, Blais S, Deschênes A, Boyle B et al. (2013) Development of high-density SNP genotyping arrays for white spruce (Picea glauca) and transferability to subtropical and nordic congeners. Mol Ecol Resour 13:324–336\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\")) with 6932 SNPs representing 6918 distinct gene loci, and the other one from using the Infinium SNP array Pmr25k with 4747 SNPs segregating for white spruce and representing 3991 gene loci, for a total of 11,328 non-redundant SNPs representing a total 8647 distinct gene loci. To retain only high-quality non-singleton SNPs, the following SNP quality criteria were applied: a genotyping reproducibility rate ≥ 95% as calculated from replicated controls, a call rate ≥90%, a fixation index |*F*e | ≤ 0.50, and a minor allele frequency (MAF) ≥ 0.01. Mendelian segregation of each SNP was also verified. For black spruce, SNPs with ≥5% of genotyping errors, that is, incompatible genotypes according to parent genotypes, or for which genotype frequencies strongly departed from Mendelian expectations in more than 3 families (Fisher's exact test, *P* < 0.001 using families with ≥10 individuals) were also discarded. For white spruce, see Nadeau et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")) for details of the filtering criteria, including Mendelian segregation, similar to those used for black spruce above. A total of two black spruce trees and one white spruce tree were discarded because their call rates were <90% (average call rate was 99%). After applying these filters for retaining only non-singleton high-quality SNPs, a total of 16,207 SNPs representing 10,894 distinct gene loci were retained for quantitative genomic analyses for black spruce, and a total of 10,289 SNPs representing 8647 distinct gene loci for quantitative genomic analyses for white spruce. For black spruce and white spruce, the retained SNPs had an average call rate per tree of 99.0%, an average genotyping reproducibility rate of 99.99% as assessed by two replicated control genotypes on each genotyping plate, and an overall average minor allele frequency MAF = 0.19 and an average fixation index *F**e* = 0.04.\n\nFor Norway spruce, the 1375 trees were genotyped for 116,765 SNPs representing 20,695 distinct gene clusters, using an exome capture and Illumina HiSeq 2500 platform (Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")). After applying several filters for retaining high-quality SNPs with a minor allele frequency MAF ≥ 0.01 with a call rate ≥90%, 116,219 SNPs were retained for analyses, as in Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")).\n\nOverall, no imputation of missing data was necessary given the high quality of the genotyping data sets obtained for each of the three species, with individual trees having a call rate in excess of 99%, on average.\n\n### Quantitative genetic analyses\n\n#### Relationship matrices\n\nTo estimate genetic parameters for broad- and narrow-sense heritability, individual-tree linear mixed models using pedigree-based or genomic-based relationship matrices for ABLUP-AD and GBLUP-AD methods, respectively, were run. The pedigree-based additive relationship matrix (\\({\\boldsymbol{A}}\\)) and its inverse were computed for each species separately using the “Amatrix” function of the R package AGHmatrix (Amadeu et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR1 \"Amadeu RR, Cellon C, Olmstead JW, Garcia AAF, Resende MFR, Muñoz PR (2016) AGHmatrix: R package to construct relationship matrices for autotetraploid and diploid species: a blueberry example. Plant Genome 9(10p):3\")) and the “G.inverse” function of the R package ASRgenomics (Gezan et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR34 \"Gezan S, de Oliveira AA, Galli G, Murray D (2022) ASRgenomics: an R package with complementary genomic functions\")), respectively. The realized additive genomic relationship matrix (\\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}\\)) was obtained using the “G.matrix” function of the R package ASRgenomics, using the formula described by VanRaden ([2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR91 \"VanRaden PM (2008) Efficient methods to compute genomic predictions. J Dairy Sci 91:4414–4423\")). To facilitate the inversion of \\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}\\), a blending of both matrices was done prior to the inversion of \\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a\\\\_blended}}}\\) such as:\n\n$${{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}{\\rm{\\\\_}}{\\boldsymbol{blended}}}=0.98\\* {{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}+0.02\\* {\\boldsymbol{A}}$$\n\n(1)\n\nThe blending was also tested with proportions of 0.05 and 0.01 for \\({\\boldsymbol{A}}\\), but did not change the results of genetic parameters (not shown). The inverse of the matrix \\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a\\\\_blended}}}\\) was obtained using the “solve” function from base R. Dominance relationship matrices were also calculated. The pedigree-based dominance relationship matrix (\\({\\boldsymbol{D}}\\)) and its inverse were calculated using the “Amatrix” function of the R package AGHmatrix and the “makeD” function of the R package nadiv (Wolak [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR96 \"Wolak ME (2012) Nadiv: an R package to create relatedness matrices for estimating non-additive genetic variances in animal models. Methods Ecol Evol 3:792–796\")), respectively. The marker-based dominance relationship matrix (\\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{d}}}\\)) was computed using the “G.matrix” function of the R package ASRgenomics following the classical parametrization as in Vitezica et al. ([2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR93 \"Vitezica ZG, Varona L, Legarra A (2013) On the additive and dominant variance and covariance of individuals within the genomic selection scope. Genetics 195:1223–1230\")), and then blended with the matrix \\({\\boldsymbol{D}}\\) as in (1) to invert it with the “G.inverse” function.\n\n#### Statistical models\n\nEvery statistical model fitted in this study was performed under ASReml-R version 4 in the R 4.3.2 environment (R Core Team [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR80 \"R. Core Team (2023) R: a language and environment for statistical computing\")). We built models for several subsets of SNPs, varying in intervals of 1000 from 500 SNPs up to the entire set of available SNPs for each species. For each SNP subset, we first created five replicates by randomly drawing SNPs from the complete set of SNPs. The variation between replicates was estimated by standard deviation. Given that the black spruce test contained some clonal replicates, only one ramet per clone was randomly chosen for each of the five replicates per level of SNP subsampling. To ensure that five replicates were sufficient per subset of SNPs tested in each species to obtain consistent standard deviation estimates among spruce species, additional resampling of up to 20 replicates was conducted for one growth trait (tree height) and one wood quality trait (wood density) in white spruce and Norway spruce, the species in this study with the lowest and highest numbers of available SNPs, respectively (see Supplementary Material, Figs. [S4](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1) and [S5](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)). Cross-validations (see below) were conducted for the various subsets of randomly drawn SNPs, i.e., also varying in 1000 SNP intervals from 500 to the full set of SNPs available for each of the replicate and for each of the three species analysed.\n\nModels included additive (A) and dominance (D) effects. In addition to marker-based models, pedigree-based models were also fitted for comparison (GBLUP-AD and ABLUP-AD, respectively).\n\nThe statistical model used for black and white spruce is defined below, was such as:\n\n$${\\boldsymbol{y}}={\\boldsymbol{X}}{\\boldsymbol{\\beta }}+{{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{1}}}{\\boldsymbol{b}}\\left({\\boldsymbol{s}}\\right){\\,+\\,{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{2}}}{\\boldsymbol{p}}\\left({\\boldsymbol{s}}\\right){\\,+\\,{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{3}}}{\\boldsymbol{a}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\,{+\\,{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{4}}}{\\boldsymbol{d}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})+{\\boldsymbol{e}}$$\n\n(2)\n\nwhere \\({\\boldsymbol{y}}\\) the vector of measured trait values (phenotypes); \\({\\boldsymbol{\\beta }}\\) is the vector of the fixed site effect, including overall mean and the site effect; \\({\\boldsymbol{b}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\) is the random block within site effect, described as heterogeneous variances between sites using a block diagonal variance-covariance structure, that is \\({\\boldsymbol{b}}({\\boldsymbol{s}}){\\boldsymbol{ \\sim }}{\\boldsymbol{N}}({\\boldsymbol{0}},{\\oplus }\\_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }\\_{b(s)i}^{2}{{\\boldsymbol{I}}}\\_{i})\\); \\({\\boldsymbol{p}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\) is the random plot effect nested within site, and described as heterogeneous variances between sites using a block diagonal variance-covariance structure, that is \\({\\boldsymbol{p}}({\\boldsymbol{s}}) \\sim {\\boldsymbol{N}}({\\boldsymbol{0}},{\\oplus }\\_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }\\_{p(s)i}^{2}{{\\boldsymbol{I}}}\\_{i})\\); \\({\\boldsymbol{a}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\) is the random additive effect nested within site described as \\({\\boldsymbol{a}}({\\boldsymbol{s}}) \\sim {\\boldsymbol{N}}({\\boldsymbol{0}},\\,{{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{a}\\otimes {{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{a})\\); \\({\\boldsymbol{d}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\) is the random dominance effect nested within site described as \\({\\boldsymbol{d}}({\\boldsymbol{s}}) \\sim {\\boldsymbol{N}}({\\boldsymbol{0}},\\,{{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{a}\\otimes {{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{a})\\); and \\({\\boldsymbol{e}}\\) is the residual error term described as heterogeneous variances between sites, that is \\({\\boldsymbol{e}}{\\boldsymbol{ \\sim }}{\\boldsymbol{N}}({\\boldsymbol{0}},{\\oplus }\\_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }\\_{e(s)i}^{2}{{\\boldsymbol{I}}}\\_{i})\\). The symbols \\(\\oplus\\) and \\(\\otimes\\) refer to the direct sum and Kronecker product of matrices, respectively.\n\nThe statistical model used for Norway spruce is defined below, such as:\n\n$${\\boldsymbol{y}}={\\boldsymbol{X}}{\\boldsymbol{\\beta }}+{{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{1}}}{\\boldsymbol{b}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\,{\\,+\\,{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{2}}}{\\boldsymbol{a}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})\\,{+\\,{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\bf{3}}}{\\boldsymbol{d}}({\\boldsymbol{s}})+{\\boldsymbol{e}}$$\n\n(3)\n\nwhere the terms were previously defined in Eq. ([2](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Equ2)).\n\n\\({\\boldsymbol{X}}\\) and \\({{\\boldsymbol{Z}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{x}}}\\) are incidence matrices of their corresponding effects. The description of the effects fitted in each model is included in Table [1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab1).\n\n**Table 1 Description of the effects included for each model tested for each dataset.**\n\n[Full size table](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/tables/1)\n\n\\({\\boldsymbol{A}}\\) and \\({\\boldsymbol{D}}\\) matrices for the ABLUP-AD methods were replaced by \\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}\\) and \\({{\\boldsymbol{G}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{d}}}\\) matrices for the GBLUP-AD method, respectively. The matrices \\({{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}\\) and \\({{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{d}}}\\) are 2 ×2 variance matrices defined by the correlation of the corresponding genetic effects between sites (\\({r}\\_{a}\\), \\({r}\\_{d}\\)) and unique variances for each site, as obtained from the CORH structure in ASReml-R. They are defined below, such as:\n\n$$\\,{{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{a}}}=\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}{{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{{{aS1}}}}^{{{2}}} & {{{{{r}}}\\_{{{a}}\\,}{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{aS1}}\\,{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{aS2}}\\\\ {{{{{r}}}\\_{{{a}}\\,}{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{aS1}}\\,{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{aS2}} & {{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{{{aS2}}}}^{{{2}}}\\end{array}\\right]{{\\boldsymbol{V}}}\\_{{\\boldsymbol{d}}}=\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{cc}{{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{{{dS1}}}}^{{{2}}} & {{{{{r}}}\\_{{{d}}\\,}{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{dS1}}\\,{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{dS2}}\\\\ {{{{{r}}}\\_{{{d}}\\,}{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{dS1}}\\,{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{dS2}} & {{{\\sigma }}}\\_{{{{dS2}}}}^{{{2}}}\\end{array}\\right]$$\n\n(4)\n\nwhere \\({{\\sigma }^{2}}\\_{{aS}1}\\) and \\({{\\sigma }^{2}}\\_{{aS}2}\\) are the additive variances of sites 1 and 2, respectively. \\({{\\sigma }^{2}}\\_{{dS}1}\\) and \\({{\\sigma }^{2}}\\_{{dS}2}\\) are the dominance variances of sites 1 and 2, respectively.\n\n#### Calculation of broad- and narrow-sense heritability estimates and their components\n\nThe across-site additive and dominance genetic variances were recovered as \\({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{a}=\\,{\\hat{r}}\\_{a}({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{aS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{aS}2})/2\\) and \\({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{d}=\\,{\\hat{r}}\\_{d}({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{dS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{dS}2})/2\\), where the subscripts S1 and S2 refer to sites 1 and 2, respectively. The formulas used for estimations of individual narrow-sense heritability (\\({{\\hat{h}}^{2}}\\_{{ind}}\\)), dominance ratio (\\({{\\hat{d}}^{2}}\\_{{ind}}\\)), and broad-sense heritability (\\({{\\hat{H}}^{2}}\\_{{ind}}\\)) are presented in Eqs. ([5](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Equ5))–([7](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Equ7)) below, such as:\n\n$${{\\hat{{{h}}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{ind}}}=\\frac{{{\\hat{{{\\sigma }}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{a}}}}{\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{p\\left(s\\right)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{a(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{d(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{e(s)}}\\,}$$\n\n(5)\n\n$${{\\hat{{{d}}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{ind}}}=\\frac{{{\\hat{{{\\sigma }}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{d}}}}{\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{p\\left(s\\right)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{a(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{d(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{e(s)}}\\,}$$\n\n(6)\n\n$${{\\hat{{{H}}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{ind}}}=\\frac{{{\\hat{{{\\sigma }}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{a}}}{\\boldsymbol{+}}\\,{{\\hat{{{\\sigma }}}}^{{{2}}}}\\_{{{d}}}}{\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{p\\left(s\\right)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{a(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{d(s)}}\\,+\\,\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{e(s)}}},$$\n\n(7)\n\nwhere \\(\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{p\\left(s\\right)}}\\)=\\(({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{pS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{pS}2})/2\\), \\(\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{a\\left(s\\right)}}\\,\\)= \\(({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{aS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{aS}2})/2\\), \\(\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{d\\left(s\\right)}}\\,\\)= \\(({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{dS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{dS}2})/2\\), and \\(\\overline{{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{e\\left(s\\right)}}\\,\\)= \\(({{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{eS}1}+{{\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2}}\\_{{eS}2})/2\\).\n\n#### Cross-validation of genomic selection models\n\nFor each dataset analysed, genotypes were randomly split into 10 folds, making sure that each fold contained 10 percent of the genotypes from each family (within-family folding, Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR51 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Azaiez A, Gérardi S, Deslauriers M, Perron M et al. (2020b) Genomic prediction for hastening and improving efficiency of forward selection in conifer polycross mating designs: an example from white spruce. Heredity 124:562–578\")). For the white spruce and Norway spruce datasets, genotypes were always associated with distinct trees. For the black spruce dataset, some genotypes were clonally replicated as indicated above. In this case, one ramet per clone was randomly chosen. This was done because the clone was not explicitly modeled as a separate random effect, and including multiple ramets of the same clone across training and validation sets could artificially inflate predictive ability due to the presence of genetically identical individuals in both sets. Cross-validation was therefore conducted at the genotype level rather than at the ramet level for this species. For each round of cross-validation, 9 folds were used to train the model used to predict the breeding or genetic values of the remaining fold. Each fold was used once as model validation. From the cross-validation models, the estimated breeding values (EBVs) were recovered from the best linear unbiased predictions (BLUPs) for each genotype associated with the additive relationship matrix. The estimated total genetic values (EGVs) were calculated as the summation, for each genotype, of the additive (EBV) and dominance (EDV) BLUPs. EBVs and EDVs were recovered at the site where the genotype was in the field. The breeding and genetic values were thus recovered at the site level. For each repetition, predictive ability (PA) and prediction accuracy (PACC) were recovered by using the predicted values of the genotypes from all the folds and calculating the correlations within each site separately (i.e., using the breeding/genetic value from the site where the genotyped tree was measured), as in Nadeau et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). Then, the PA and PACC were averaged over sites and repetitions.\n\nThe predictive ability was calculated as the Pearson correlation coefficient between the predicted breeding values (\\({\\mathrm{PA}}\\_{\\mathrm{BV}}\\); for the models with additive or additive and dominance effects) or predicted genetic values (\\({\\mathrm{PA}}\\_{\\mathrm{GV}}\\); for the models with additive and dominance effects only) of the validation trees and the observed phenotypes. The prediction accuracy of breeding values (\\({\\mathrm{PACC}}\\_{\\mathrm{BV}}\\)) was estimated as the correlation between breeding values predicted in cross-validation and the corresponding breeding values obtained from the full model, including all individuals, which were used as proxies for true breeding values. Similarly, the prediction accuracy of genetic values (\\({\\mathrm{PACC}}\\_{\\mathrm{GV}}\\)) was estimated as the correlation between predicted genetic values from cross-validation and the genetic values obtained from the full model. The narrow-sense and broad-sense heritability estimates were recovered from the full models and calculated for their respective site.\n\nResults\n-------\n\nThe average number of available SNPs per gene locus differed markedly among species (Table [2](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab2)). Norway spruce exhibited a substantially higher SNP redundancy per gene locus (5.6 SNPs per locus) compared with black spruce (1.5) and white spruce (1.19). This indicates a much denser sampling of markers within genes in Norway spruce.\n\n**Table 2 Summary of the numbers of SNPs available and SNP redundancy per gene locus across the three spruce species analysed.**\n\n[Full size table](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/tables/2)\n\n### Comparison between GBLUP-AD and ABLUP-AD models for the genetic parameter estimates\n\nWith the aim of comparing trends in heritability estimates and GS model accuracy across all species and trait combinations, we retained very similar models for analyses and comparisons. Initial results had revealed significant variation in site-specific estimates, highlighting the necessity of fitting heterogeneous variances across environments on the different design and genetic effects (Tables [3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab3) and [S1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)–[S3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)).\n\n**Table 3 Across-site genetic parameters were estimated using the ABLUP-AD or the GBLUP-AD model, including all SNPs available for a species (numbers in parentheses indicate standard errors).**\n\n[Full size table](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/tables/3)\n\nFor white and Norway spruces, wood quality traits, namely acoustic velocity and wood density, were moderately to highly heritable at the narrow-sense and broad-sense levels, and this was either for the pedigree-based ABLUP-AD or the marker-based GBLUP-AD method; whereas for growth traits, namely tree height and DBH, they were under lower to moderate genetic control (Tables [3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab3) and [S1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)–[S3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)). However, the trend was slightly different in black spruce, where all three wood and growth traits were under moderate to high genetic control. Overall, generally higher broad-sense and narrow-sense heritability estimates were observed for the black spruce dataset compared with the Norway spruce and white spruce datasets.\n\nType-B genetic correlations were weaker for most growth traits, indicating more important genotype-by-environment interactions for this category of traits; while stronger genetic correlations were recorded for wood quality traits, in line with the generally higher heritability estimates obtained for this category of traits. Also, growth traits showed significant dominance effects, whereas these effects were usually less pronounced for wood quality traits. These findings underscore the distinct genetic architectures between growth and wood quality traits, and they emphasize the role of site-specific interactions in shaping trait expression in a heterogenous manner.\n\nFor the reporting of the overall trends, we focus here on the across-site heritability estimates.\n\n### Influence of marker density on heritability estimates\n\nThe repetitive sampling of SNP subsets and their use in GS models (GBLUP-AD) allowed describing trends in heritability estimates, model predictive ability, and prediction accuracy as a function of number of markers used, and comparing with the estimates obtained from models using all available markers and those with pedigree information only (ABLUP-AD) for the various growth and wood quality traits in the three studied species. In this study, growth traits included tree height and diameter at breast height (DBH), whereas wood quality traits included wood density and acoustic velocity.\n\nThe identification of stabilization levels of genetic parameter estimates with increasing marker density, also termed plateau regions below, was based on visual inspection of the graphic trends across increasing marker densities, where no formal breakpoint or quantitative threshold of plateaus was applied, given the variety of traits and species analysed. As a general trend, narrow-sense heritability and the dominance ratio of growth and wood quality traits appeared to rapidly increase from between 500 and 4000 markers (Figs. [1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig1)–[3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig3)). However, starting from about 4000 SNPs, the rate of change in narrow-sense heritability and dominance estimates became substantially lower compared to the sharp rise in trend observed before this threshold. Heritability reached a plateau after 8000 SNPs for black spruce and white spruce, and at a slightly higher level in Norway spruce. It is also notable that the reduction of the standard deviation of estimates after 4000 SNPs was also much limited. Thus, the marginal improvement of heritability estimates between 4000 and 8000 SNPs does not seem to justify efforts to double marker density unless this is useful to improve modeling accuracy significantly (see “Discussion”). Estimates of variance and dominance ratios were initially overestimated for low marker densities but gradually stabilized as marker density increased, following a trend similar to that observed for narrow- and broad-sense heritability estimates. This indicates that the marker density required for stable estimates of dominance was comparable to that required for stable estimates of additive and total genetic variance.\n\n**Fig. 1: Trends in heritability estimates for black spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, DBH, and wood density.**\n\n![Fig. 1: Trends in heritability estimates for black spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, DBH, and wood density.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig1_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/1)\n\nBroad-sense heritability and its components, the narrow-sense heritability and dominance ratio, are shown in horizontal panels. Solid lines present the mean estimates of SNP resampling of GBLUP-AD models, including the standard deviations. The dashed lines represent estimates from the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD). DBH is the tree diameter at breast height.\n\n**Fig. 2: Trends in heritability estimates for white spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, DBH, acoustic velocity, and wood density.**\n\n![Fig. 2: Trends in heritability estimates for white spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, DBH, acoustic velocity, and wood density.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig2_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/2)\n\nBroad-sense heritability and its components, the narrow-sense heritability and dominance ratio, are shown in horizontal panels. Solid lines present the mean estimates of SNP resampling of GBLUP-AD models, including the standard deviations. The dashed lines represent estimates from the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD). DBH is the tree diameter at breast height.\n\n**Fig. 3: Trends in heritability estimates for Norway spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, acoustic velocity, and wood density.**\n\n![Fig. 3: Trends in heritability estimates for Norway spruce in function of the number of markers used in GS modeling of tree height, acoustic velocity, and wood density.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig3_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/3)\n\nBroad-sense heritability and its components, the narrow-sense heritability and dominance ratio, are shown in horizontal panels. Solid lines present the mean estimates of SNP resampling of GBLUP-AD models, including the standard deviations. For better readability, a logarithmic scale is used in this figure for the number of SNPs in marker subsets. The dashed lines represent estimates from the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD). DBH is the tree diameter at breast height.\n\nA few exceptions were observed; notably, a slight increase of broad-sense heritability estimates was detected beyond 10,000 SNPs for acoustic velocity in white spruce (Fig. [2](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig2)), which was linked to a small increase in additive genetic variance estimates, although this increase remained marginal in the range of a few percent improvement. For Norway spruce (Fig. [3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig3)), the rise of heritability estimates beyond a few thousand markers was also minimal. From 25,000 SNPs onwards, the heritability estimates remained stable up to 100,000 SNPs. In this Norway spruce dataset, heritability estimates for tree height were consistently very low, regardless of the size of SNP subsets tested.\n\nIn contrast, broad-sense heritability was less variable at low marker density, compared with the respective dominance component and narrow-sense heritability estimates in all three species. The estimated dominance ratio using GBLUP-AD was initially quite high when low marker density was considered in GS models, likely reflecting confounding effects between additive and dominance variance components. As marker density increased, this confounding effect appeared to diminish, and the dominance ratio decreased, ultimately converging toward the dominance ratio observed in ABLUP-AD models. Models based on low marker density were apparently less performant in separating additive from dominance effects. Levelling out of estimates from GBLUP-AD models at higher numbers of SNPs went along with reduced standard deviations and a slight rise of parameter estimates after 4000 SNPs, and virtually no change after 8000 SNPs.\n\n### Effects of marker density on predictive ability and prediction accuracy\n\nThe trends of mean predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and of genetic values (PAGV) as a function of the number of SNPs used in GS models are illustrated in Fig. [4](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig4) for black spruce, and for white spruce and Norway spruce, in Figs. [5](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig5) and [6](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Fig6), respectively. Across all species, an increase in predictive ability was observed as marker density increased, for both the predictive ability of breeding values and genetic values. Mean estimates tended to increase to a maximum between 4000 and 8000 SNPs, and remain stable thereafter. Similarly, as for the trends in heritability estimates in Norway spruce, a minimal increase of predictive ability was observed beyond 4000 SNPs, reaching a plateau at around 25,000 SNPs.\n\n**Fig. 4: Trends in average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in black spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying subsets of SNPs.**\n\n![Fig. 4: Trends in average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in black spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying subsets of SNPs.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig4_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/4)\n\nFor the three phenotypic traits, the means and standard deviations are shown for a ten-fold cross-validation of ten times resampled marker subsets. Horizontal dashed lines represent the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD), independent of SNP number. DBH is the tree diameter at breast height.\n\n**Fig. 5: Trends of average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in white spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying subsets of SNPs.**\n\n![Fig. 5: Trends of average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in white spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying subsets of SNPs.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig5_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/5)\n\nFor the four phenotypic traits, the means and standard deviations are shown for a ten-fold cross-validation of ten times resampled marker subsets. Horizontal dashed lines represent the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD), independent of SNP number. DBH is the tree diameter at breast height.\n\n**Fig. 6: Trends in average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in Norway spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying numbers of SNPs.**\n\n![Fig. 6: Trends in average predictive ability of breeding values (PABV) and genetic values (PAGV) in Norway spruce GS models (GBLUP-AD) considering varying numbers of SNPs.](//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41437-026-00836-7/MediaObjects/41437_2026_836_Fig6_HTML.png)\n\n[Full size image](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7/figures/6)\n\nFor the three phenotypic traits, the means and standard deviation are shown for a ten-fold cross-validation of ten times resampled marker subsets. Horizontal dashed lines represent the corresponding pedigree-based models (ABLUP-AD), independent of the number of SNPs. The number of SNPs is presented on a logarithmic scale to facilitate the readability and interpretability of results obtained for smaller marker subsets.\n\nTo provide additional context, we compared these PA trends with Prediction Accuracy (PACC) estimates for breeding values (\\({\\mathrm{PACC}}\\_{\\mathrm{BV}}\\)) and genetic values (\\({\\mathrm{PACC}}\\_{\\mathrm{GV}}\\)), presented in Supplementary Figs. [S1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)–[S3](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1). Overall, the trends in PACC mirrored those observed for PA, showing similar increases with marker density and stabilization at comparable SNP density levels. This indicates that the visual patterns of PA observed in the main figures reliably reflect the underlying prediction accuracy for both additive and total genetic values. In the interest of describing trends in function of marker density used in GS models, we focus primarily on the trends in predictive ability estimates below.\n\nIn a very few cases, a slight decrease in predictive ability was observed around 2000 or 4000 SNPs despite the general increasing trend. This was so for DBH in black spruce and white spruce, although these estimates were characterized by relatively large standard deviations overlapping with the mean estimates of predictive ability obtained for the neighbouring smaller and larger SNP densities tested.\n\nIn general, the predictive ability of GS models tended to be higher or very close to pedigree-based predictive ability estimates. Wood density in Norway spruce presented an exception, as well as growth traits in white spruce, where the pedigree-based predictive ability was greater than that obtained with the genomic models (GBLUP-AD). Similarly, as for trends in heritability estimates, predictive ability of genetic values, i.e., including additive and dominance effects (PAGV), tended to vary less than their breeding value counterparts that only considered additive effects. This trend again hints towards the insufficiency of GS models relying on low marker densities to separate well additive and dominance effects, and it underlines the need to consider non-additive effects in GS modelling whenever possible.\n\nDiscussion\n----------\n\nDuring the last two decades, the determination of EST and the progress in RNA- and DNA-based sequencing methods, along with the development of automated computing pipelines to detect and filter single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) from gene sequences, has allowed the creation and functional annotation of significant marker resources for spruces (Pavy et al. [2006](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR70 \"Pavy N, Parsons L, Paule C, Mackay J, Bousquet J (2006) Automated SNP detection from a large collection of white spruce expressed sequences: contributing factors and approaches for the categorization of SNPs. BMC Genomics 7(14p):174\"), [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR75 \"Pavy N, Gagnon F, Deschênes A, Boyle B, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Development of highly reliable in silico SNP resource and genotyping assay from exome capture and sequencing: an example from black spruce (Picea mariana). Mol Ecol Resour 16:588–598\"), [2025](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR77 \"Pavy N, Gérardi S, Prunier J, Rigault P, Laroche J, Daigle G et al. (2025) Contrasting transcriptome-wide SNP diversity and adaptive molecular variation among conifers. Front Plant Sci 16(17p):1500759\"); Chen et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR20 \"Chen J, Källman T, Ma X, Gyllenstrand N, Zaina G, Morgante M et al. (2012) Disentangling the role of history and local selection in shaping clinal variation of allele frequencies and gene expression in Norway spruce (Picea abies). Genetics 191:865–881\"); Nystedt et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR66 \"Nystedt B, Street NR, Wetterbom A, Zuccolo A, Lin YC, Scofield DG et al. (2013) The Norway spruce genome sequence and conifer genome evolution. Nature 497:579–584\"); De La Torre et al. [2014](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR25 \"De La Torre AR, Birol I, Bousquet J, Ingvarsson P, Jansson S, Jones SJM et al. (2014) Insights into conifer giga-genomes. Plant Physiol 166:1724–1732\"); Azaiez et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR2 \"Azaiez A, Pavy N, Gérardi S, Laroche J, Boyle B, Gagnon F et al. (2018) A catalog of annotated high-confidence SNPs from exome capture and sequencing reveals highly polymorphic genes in Norway spruce (Picea abies). BMC Genomics 19(13p):942\"); Bernhardsson et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR11 \"Bernhardsson C, Wang X, Eklöf H, Ingvarsson PK (2019) Variant calling using NGS and sequence capture data for population and evolutionary genomic inferences in Norway Spruce (Picea abies). bioRxiv \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/805994\n                  \n                \"); Gagalova et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR30 \"Gagalova KK, Warren RL, Coombe L, Wong J, Nip KM, Yuen MM, Saint et al. (2022) Spruce giga-genomes: structurally similar yet distinctive with differentially expanding gene families and rapidly evolving genes. Plant J 111:1469–1485\"); Lo et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR57 \"Lo T, Coombe L, Gagalova KK, Marr A, Warren RL, Kirk H et al. (2024) Assembly and annotation of the black spruce giga-genome provide insights on spruce phylogeny and evolution of stress response. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 14(16p):jkad247\")). Those resources have permitted the construction of genetic maps (Pelgas et al. [2006](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR79 \"Pelgas B, Beauseigle S, Acheré V, Jeandroz S, Bousquet J, Isabel N (2006) Comparative genome mapping among Picea glauca, P. mariana × P. rubens and P. abies, and correspondence with other Pinaceae. Theor Appl Genet 113:1371–1393\"), [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR78 \"Pelgas B, Bousquet J, Meirmans PG, Ritland K, Isabel N (2011) QTL mapping in white spruce: gene maps and genomic regions underlying adaptive traits across pedigrees, years and environments. BMC Genomics 12(23p):145\"); Lind et al. [2014](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR56 \"Lind M, Källman T, Chen J, Ma X-F, Bousquet J, Morgante M et al. (2014) A Picea abies linkage map based on SNP markers identifies QTLs for four aspects of resistance to Heterobasidion parviporum infection. PLoS ONE 9(16p):e101049\"); Pavy et al. 2012, [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR76 \"Pavy N, Lamothe M, Pelgas B, Gagnon F, Birol I, Bohlmann J et al. (2017) A high-resolution reference genetic map positioning 8.8 K genes for the conifer white spruce: structural genomics implications and correspondence with physical distance. Plant J 90:189–203\"); Bernhardsson et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR11 \"Bernhardsson C, Wang X, Eklöf H, Ingvarsson PK (2019) Variant calling using NGS and sequence capture data for population and evolutionary genomic inferences in Norway Spruce (Picea abies). bioRxiv \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/805994\n                  \n                \"); Gagalova et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR30 \"Gagalova KK, Warren RL, Coombe L, Wong J, Nip KM, Yuen MM, Saint et al. (2022) Spruce giga-genomes: structurally similar yet distinctive with differentially expanding gene families and rapidly evolving genes. Plant J 111:1469–1485\")) as well as the creation of new genotyping tools for breeding applications in white spruce (Pavy et al. [2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR72 \"Pavy N, Pelgas B, Beauseigle S, Blais S, Gagnon F, Gosselin I et al. (2008) Enhancing genetic mapping of complex genomes through the design of highly-multiplexed SNP arrays: application to the large and unsequenced genomes of white spruce and black spruce. BMC Genomics 9(17p):21\"), [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR74 \"Pavy N, Gagnon F, Rigault P, Blais S, Deschênes A, Boyle B et al. (2013) Development of high-density SNP genotyping arrays for white spruce (Picea glauca) and transferability to subtropical and nordic congeners. Mol Ecol Resour 13:324–336\"); Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\")), black spruce (Pavy et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR75 \"Pavy N, Gagnon F, Deschênes A, Boyle B, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Development of highly reliable in silico SNP resource and genotyping assay from exome capture and sequencing: an example from black spruce (Picea mariana). Mol Ecol Resour 16:588–598\")), and Norway spruce (Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"); Bernhardsson et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR10 \"Bernhardsson C, Zan Y, Chen Z, Ingvarsson PK, Wu HX (2021) Development of a highly efficient 50K single nucleotide polymorphism genotyping array for the large and complex genome of Norway spruce (Picea abies L. Karst) by whole genome resequencing and its transferability to other spruce species. Mol Ecol Resour 21:880–896\")). Still, the use of many thousands of markers for the construction of SNP genotyping arrays and the subsequent genotyping of thousands of trees is usually associated to significant costs. Consequently, it is perceived by some tree breeders as a financial burden to the large-scale application of GS in tree breeding programs (Grattapaglia [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR37 \"Grattapaglia D (2022) Twelve years into genomic selection in forest trees: climbing the slope of enlightenment of marker assisted tree breeding. Forests 13(25p):10\")), perhaps ignoring the large-scale benefits of GS per unit of time to accelerate the deployment of improved varieties in reforestation programs and improved plantations (Chang et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR19 \"Chang WY, Gaston C, Cool J, Thomas BR (2019) A financial analysis of using improved planting stock of white spruce and lodge pole pine in Alberta, Canada: genomic selection versus traditional breeding. Forestry 92:297–310\"); Chamberland et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR18 \"Chamberland V, Robichaud F, Perron M, Gélinas N, Bousquet J, Beaulieu J (2020) Conventional versus genomic selection for white spruce improvement: a comparison of costs and benefits of plantations on Quebec public lands. Tree Genet Genomes 16(16p):17\")), which are downstream benefits often not considered in the funding of tree breeding programs.\n\nBased on three major datasets, the results of the present study appear as a significant step forward to inform on the minimum number of markers needed for most exact estimation of genetic parameters such as heritability and the precision of GS models for the prediction of breeding values, at least in conifer species with very large giga-genomes such as for spruces (see Beaulieu et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")), Table [1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab1)). Furthermore, a well-defined selection of marker panels appears essential for operational decisions regarding genotyping for GS applications, as breeding program managers may have to balance the accuracy of parameter estimates and associated costs for genotyping. Although metadata analysis and simulation studies such as Grattapaglia and Resende ([2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) and Beaulieu et al. ([2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\"), 2004) have highlighted key elements impacting the precision of GS models and genetic parameter estimates, comprehensive empirical studies that demonstrate these effects at the early stage of tree breeding programs are still scarce.\n\nWe focused our analyses on broad- and narrow-sense heritability to assess the models’ capacity to estimate and separate additive and dominance effects, as well as their effects on predictive ability (PA) and prediction accuracy (PACC) to evaluate the ability of GS models to accurately predict breeding and genetic values using a variety of marker densities. For all three spruce species, general estimates based on pedigree information, or based on all available markers, were in the same range as those reported in earlier homologous studies by Lenz et al. ([2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")) for black spruce, Beaulieu et al. ([2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\")) for white spruce, and Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) for Norway spruce. Some mostly minor differences were related to the fact that different algorithms and models were used. The present study relied on a linear mixed model framework (GBLUP) as it represents a straightforward way to consider heterogenous error and genetic variances across different environments, and to include dominance effects in these controlled-cross populations made of related full-sib families. For black spruce and white spruce, many more markers were available than in previous studies, which more than doubled the number of SNP gene markers initially used in previous studies (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\") and Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"), respectively), thus allowing for reaching plateaus in estimates for most studied parameters derived from GS, as for Norway spruce.\n\n### Effect of marker density on genetic parameter estimates\n\nThe subsampling approach used herein to randomly create subsets of markers differing in numbers allowed us to obtain the trends in heritability, PA, and PACC estimates with increasing marker density. The general trends were largely mirrored across the three species, despite some differences in experimental setups and age of trait measurements among species. Heritability and predictive ability increased significantly among subsets varying from 500 to around 4000 SNPs, and low improvement, or rather stagnation of estimates, was observed beyond. Additionally, the standard deviation of estimates decreased as the number of SNPs increased, reinforcing the notion that reliable heritability estimates are highly dependent on the quality and size of the SNP dataset.\n\nIt is difficult to conclude about the reason for the slightly later culmination of heritability and accuracy estimates in Norway spruce around 25,000 SNPs, as this could be related to the different experimental design. Given the more intensive sampling of SNPs per gene locus to build the Norway spruce SNP array (Vidalis et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR92 \"Vidalis A, Scofield DG, Neves LG, Bernhardsson C, García-Gil MR, Ingvarsson PK (2018) Design and evaluation of a large sequence-capture probe set and associated SNPs for diploid and haploid samples of Norway spruce (Picea abies). bioRxiv 2018: \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/291716\n                  \n                \"); Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) than for the other two species, on average, genes with many SNPs arrayed were more likely to be retained in the small subsets of SNPs that those represented by few SNPs, sometimes in a redundant manner, thus reducing marker density in terms of distinct sampled gene loci compared to black spruce or white spruce, thus with less redundant SNPs per genetic locus on their SNP array. But also, the Norway spruce dataset contained more parents and families (thus a higher status number) but a smaller number of trees tested per family, than in the black spruce or white spruce datasets (Supplementary Table [S4](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#MOESM1)). This may have led to less precise estimation of genetic variance at lower values of marker density tested.\n\nIn line with this, a recent meta-analysis of empirical studies (Beaulieu et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")) and early simulation studies (Grattapaglia and Resende [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) reported a negative relationship between status number and predictive ability such that, as a general trend, breeding values could be more precisely estimated in populations of smaller effective size for about the same range of number of trees considered. It was also shown that no relationship between heritability estimates and parameters such as the number of markers or the size of the training set in the range of values available from the literature (Beaulieu et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")). This observation also underlines that heritability is a site- and population-specific measure, meaning that broad trends can be more difficult to detect when analysing data from varied population structures and study designs. These findings suggest caution when extrapolating results from the present study to very different genetic structures of breeding populations, such as for breeding programs based on half-sib families from natural populations, which usually harbor much larger census and effective sizes than populations of related full-sib families, such as analysed here for these three spruce species (see also Beaulieu et al. ([2014a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR6 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014a) Accuracy of genomic selection models in a large population of open-pollinated families in white spruce. Heredity 113:343–352\")) vs. ([2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\")) for comparison of GS accuracy in the context of half-sib versus full-sib population structure in same species with similar GS modeling.\n\nEarlier studies had already indicated that low marker density, in the range of a few thousand markers, can lead to reasonable prediction accuracy of genomic-estimated breeding values in the context of related full-sib families and small effective sizes (Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")). The resampling approach used in this study also allows generalizing such observations made on Norway spruce by Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")). An alternative approach to improve model performance at low marker densities is to combine pedigree- and marker-based relationship matrices using a weighted blending strategy, where the contribution of the pedigree matrix may be greater at low SNP densities and negligible at high densities. The findings that available total marker panels were not necessary to achieve precise estimates of genetic parameters and GS accuracy underscore the diminishing returns of adding more SNPs beyond a certain threshold, emphasizing the importance of an optimal balance considering dataset size, population structure, and GS accuracy. Similar results were reported in *Eucalyptus benthamii* Maiden & Cambage, a broadleaf tree species with much smaller physical genome than spruces, where Estopa et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR28 \"Estopa RA, Paludeto JGZ, Müller BSF, de Oliveira RA, Azevedo CF, de Resende MDV et al. (2023) Genomic prediction of growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii using different genomic models and variable SNP genotyping density. New For 54:343–362\")) showed that a similarly reduced number of SNPs (less than 10,000 for most traits) was sufficient to obtain good predictive ability and accurate genetic parameter estimates estimates for growth and wood quality traits. Our findings are also consistent with reports on the influence of marker density on genomic prediction accuracy in plant and animal breeding, such as in alfalfa (Wang et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR94 \"Wang H, Bai Y, Biligetu B (2024) Effects of SNP marker density and training population size on prediction accuracy in alfalfa (Medicago sativa L.) genomic selection. Plant Genome 17(19p):e20431\")), maize (Guo et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR39 \"Guo R, Dhliwayo T, Mageto EK, Palacios-Rojas N, Lee M, Yu D et al. (2020) Genomic prediction of kernel zinc concentration in multiple maize populations using genotyping-by-sequencing and repeat amplification sequencing markers. Front Plant Sci 11:534\")), wheat (Norman et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR65 \"Norman A, Taylor J, Edwards J, Kuchel H (2018) Optimising genomic selection in wheat: effect of marker density, population size and population structure on prediction accuracy. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 8:2889–2899\")), cattle and pigs (Zhang et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR99 \"Zhang H, Yin L, Wang M, Yuan X, Liu X (2019) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for agricultural economic traits in maize, cattle, and pig populations. Front Genet 10(10p):189\")). Those studies showed that increasing the number of markers led to higher prediction accuracy, but only up to certain thresholds beyond which additional markers provided limited enhancement. This pattern mirrors observations from trend analysis of results gathered from many GS studies in forest tree species, where a significant relationship was not observed between marker density and GS accuracy in the range of available marker densities used in published GS studies on trees (Beaulieu et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")). Altogether, these studies suggest the relevance of identifying a minimum marker density that balances genotyping costs with genetic parameter and prediction accuracy, particularly when considering that the application of GS is often resource-limited tree breeding programs.\n\nAs reported by Estopa et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR28 \"Estopa RA, Paludeto JGZ, Müller BSF, de Oliveira RA, Azevedo CF, de Resende MDV et al. (2023) Genomic prediction of growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii using different genomic models and variable SNP genotyping density. New For 54:343–362\")) for *E. benthamii*, the earlier culmination and plateau of values reached for PA in relation to an increasing number of markers, compared to that for heritability estimates, was not observed as a clear trend in the present study on three spruce species. Measures of the GS model precision, such as PA, followed closely trends in heritability estimates, which necessitated a marginally lower marker density for traits with low heritability to attain plateaus of values. Given that quantitative traits with weak or weaker genetic control have a smaller proportion of their phenotypic variation explained by genetic effects, these traits might necessitate less marker density for their heritability to be traced adequately, and most QTLs bear small gene effects, such as those in relation to adaptive traits (Pelgas et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR78 \"Pelgas B, Bousquet J, Meirmans PG, Ritland K, Isabel N (2011) QTL mapping in white spruce: gene maps and genomic regions underlying adaptive traits across pedigrees, years and environments. BMC Genomics 12(23p):145\")). Further studies are needed on comparative genomic architecture between quantitative traits with low versus high heritability in tree species.\n\nFinally, it should be noted that the present estimates of heritability, predictive ability (PA), and prediction accuracy (PACC) were based on cross-validation, which may slightly overestimate these parameters. Therefore, the observed marker density thresholds to attain plateau values of genetic parameters, such as around 4000 SNPs, should be considered as indicative rather than in absolute terms.\n\n### Higher marker density helped disentangle additive from dominance genetic effects\n\nPedigree-based models tended to result in slightly higher heritability estimates than those derived from GS models, which was especially so for narrow-sense heritability estimates. For example, for black spruce growth traits, narrow-sense heritability estimates were higher in pedigree-based models compared to their marker-based counterparts. At the same time, those higher estimates for pedigree-based models did not lead to higher or significantly higher PABV values. Similar findings were reported by Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) for wood quality traits in Norway spruce, where the pedigree-based ABLUP models also produced higher heritability estimates. This result may be explained by the fact that the pedigree-based models can overestimate additive variance, and consequently, these heritability estimates do not rely on actual genetic relationships (Beaulieu et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\")). Comparable results were observed in *E. benthamii* (Estopa et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR28 \"Estopa RA, Paludeto JGZ, Müller BSF, de Oliveira RA, Azevedo CF, de Resende MDV et al. (2023) Genomic prediction of growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii using different genomic models and variable SNP genotyping density. New For 54:343–362\")), in black spruce (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")), and in a meta-analysis of GS applied in various tree species (Beaulieu et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\"), [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")). GS models are thus expected to provide more accurate estimates of variance components and heritability compared to pedigree-based models, especially when plateaus of values have been reached. Indeed, pedigree-based methods overlook genetic relationships beyond those recorded in the registered pedigree and thus, rely on expected rather than actual genetic relationships. Furthermore, these models assume homogeneity in relationships among the same types of parents, which means that genetic covariance components are estimated primarily from interfamilial variation. This approach has limitations because Mendelian sampling deviations cannot be distinguished from non-genetic residual errors (Ødegård and Meuwissen [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR67 \"Ødegård J, Meuwissen THE (2012) Estimation of heritability from limited family data using genome-wide identity-by-descent sharing. Genet Sel Evol 44(10p):16\")). Moreover, if errors exist in the registered pedigree, they cannot be corrected, contrary to GBLUP, for instance (Godbout et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR35 \"Godbout J, Tremblay L, Levasseur C, Lavigne P, Rainville A, Mackay J et al. (2017) Development of a traceability system based on a SNP array for large-scale production of high-value white spruce (Picea glauca). Front Plant Sci 8(13p):1264\"); Li et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR55 \"Li Y, Klápště J, Telfer E, Wilcox P, Graham N, MacDonald L et al. (2019) Genomic selection for non-key traits in radiata pine when the documented pedigree is corrected using DNA marker information. BMC Genomics 20(10p):1026\")).\n\nExperimental studies have shown that incorporating dominance effects into genomic models reduces the estimated additive genetic variance and narrow-sense heritability (De Almeida Filho et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR24 \"De Almeida Filho JE, Guimarães JFR, Fonsceca E, Silva F, De Resende MDV, Muñoz P, Kirst M et al. (2019) Genomic prediction of additive and non-additive effects using genetic markers and pedigrees. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 9:2739–2748\"); Paludeto et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR68 \"Paludeto JGZ, Grattapaglia D, Estopa RA, Tambarussi EV (2021) Genomic relationship-based genetic parameters and prospects of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii. Tree Genet Genomes 17(20p):38\"); Nadeau et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). Indeed, dominance effects represent a portion of genetic variance that appears to be less well-captured by pedigree-based models than marker-based models. This reduction occurred because, in models excluding dominance, part of the dominance variance is spuriously captured by the additive component, and is reassigned to dominance when these effects are explicitly modeled (Vitezica et al. [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR93 \"Vitezica ZG, Varona L, Legarra A (2013) On the additive and dominant variance and covariance of individuals within the genomic selection scope. Genetics 195:1223–1230\")). By better capturing dominant genetic components, GS models provide more accurate estimates of additive variance, often leading to lower heritability estimates compared to pedigree-based models (Beaulieu et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR4 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz P, Bousquet J (2022) Metadata analysis indicates biased estimation of genetic parameters using conventional pedigree information instead of genomic-based approaches in tree breeding. Sci Rep 12(10p):3933\")). This improvement in accuracy highlights the importance of accounting for dominance effects in genetic analysis, whenever possible.\n\nFurthermore, almost all GS models lead to the highest PAGV values when dominance deviation was included in the prediction of genetic values. This trend is also supported by other studies in white spruce (Nadeau et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")). For spruce and tree breeders aiming to estimate genetic parameters of their breeding populations, that is, including both additive and dominance effects, our results indicate that this can also be done at relatively low marker densities, between 4000 SNPs and 8000 SNPs. At the lowest end of this range, however, additive and dominance variances do not appear to be well disentangled, which may lead to less precise estimates of genetic values and suboptimal selection of breeding and reproduction material. Nevertheless, PAGV values were already high at quite low marker density, indicating that genetic values can already be estimated with sufficient accuracy with quite low marker densities. But this is of rather restricted use for several conifer breeding programs, given that only a few programs apply clonal reproduction by rooted cuttings or somatic embryogenesis (SE) for the production of improved varieties, where non-additive genetic effects such as dominance can indeed be considered and exploited for additional genetic gains (Park et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR69 \"Park Y-S, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Multi-varietal forestry integrating genomic selection and somatic embryogenesis. In: Park Y-S, Bonga J, Moon HK (eds) Vegetative propagation of forest trees. National Institute of Forest Science, Seoul, South Korea, pp 302–322\")).\n\n### Genomic distribution of markers and genome architecture\n\nOur results indicated that only moderate marker densities are needed to attain high precision of genetic parameter estimates and accuracy of GS models for an array of functional traits, as applied to spruce breeding populations of relatively small status number typical of advanced-breeding populations. One must remember that such breeding populations are characterized by high LD, contrary to outcrossing natural populations of forest trees, usually with large effective population sizes (Bouillé and Bousquet [2005](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR13 \"Bouillé M, Bousquet J (2005) Trans-species shared polymorphisms at orthologous nuclear gene loci among distant species in the conifer Picea (Pinaceae): implications for the long-term maintenance of genetic diversity in trees. Am J Bot 92(1):63–73\")), where LD decays rapidly, often well within gene limits (Neale and Savolainen [2004](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR64 \"Neale DB, Savolainen O (2004) Association genetics of complex traits in conifers. Trends Plant Sci 9:325–330\"); Pavy et al. [2012b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR71 \"Pavy N, Namroud M-C, Gagnon F, Isabel N, Bousquet J (2012b) The heterogeneous levels of linkage disequilibrium in white spruce genes and comparative analysis with other conifers. Heredity 108:273–284\")). For instance, in genome-wide association studies (GWAS) such as the detection of genotype-phenotype associations (GPA) or genotype-environmental associations (GEA), where significant associations are mostly determined by short-range LD between markers and QTLs, unstructured populations or structured populations stripped of most of their genetic structure should usually be used in GWAS to avoid artifactual results (e.g., Hornoy et al. [2015](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR47 \"Hornoy B, Pavy N, Gérardi S, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2015) Genetic adaptation to climate in white spruce involves small to moderate allele frequency shifts in functionally diverse genes. Genome Biol Evol 7:3269–3285\"); Depardieu et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR27 \"Depardieu C, Gérardi S, Nadeau S, Parent G, Mackay J, Lenz P et al. (2021) Connecting tree-ring phenotypes, genetic associations and transcriptomics to decipher the genomic architecture of drought adaptation in a widespread conifer. Mol Ecol 30:3898–3917\")). In such cases, very large numbers of markers and genetic loci need to be screened to identify significant associations, given the rapid decay of LD in these populations. In breeding populations where GS is usually applied, especially those made of related families and of reduced status number as in advanced-breeding generations, they are more highly structured with high LD, making GS most accurate while relying largely on relatedness instead of short-range LD between markers and QTLs (Habier et al. [2007](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR40 \"Habier D, Fernando RL, Dekkers JCM (2007) The impact of genetic relationship information on genome-assisted breeding values. Genet Soc Am 177:2389–2397\"); Zapata-Valenzuela et al. [2012](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR98 \"Zapata-Valenzuela J, Isik F, Maltecca C, Wegrzyn J, Neale D, McKeand S et al. (2012) SNP markers trace familial linkages in a cloned population of Pinus taeda—prospects for genomic selection. Tree Genet Genomes 8:1307–1318\"); Beaulieu et al. [2014b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR5 \"Beaulieu J, Doerksen T, Clément S, MacKay J, Bousquet J (2014b) Genomic selection accuracies within and between environments and small breeding groups in white spruce. BMC Genomics 15(16p):1048\"); Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"); Grattapaglia et al. 2022).\n\nWhile GS model accuracy largely depended on the number of markers used in the context of capturing well relatedness, it is also likely that the number of distinct genetic loci considered in SNP array development or any other genotyping method, and their distribution on the genome, also plays a significant role in the efficiency of the marker density used to capture relatedness with more reduced numbers of markers. Indeed, the white spruce and black spruce SNP arrays used herein relied on maximizing as much as possible the number of distinct gene loci on the array so as to improve genome coverage for the same number of markers, thus with a much reduced SNP redundancy of less than two SNPs per gene locus, on average (Table [2](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab2)). As a test control, many more SNPs per gene locus could be tested for Norway spruce, without clear improvement of GS accuracy well beyond the number of gene clusters considered for SNP detection (around 20,000). This trend indicates that SNPs within the same genes were generally in high LD in such structured breeding populations of small status number, with on average diminishing GS returns of the individual informative value of each SNP when being redundant in the same gene loci.\n\nA quite even distribution of markers onto the genome also appears important, which is often an information not available for many tree species. For instance, in the present study, gene loci sampled in each species for SNP genotyping were distributed exome-wide, with previous studies indicating that the exome covers quite uniformly the 12 chromosome-wide linkage groups of spruce sp. (Pavy et al. [2008](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR72 \"Pavy N, Pelgas B, Beauseigle S, Blais S, Gagnon F, Gosselin I et al. (2008) Enhancing genetic mapping of complex genomes through the design of highly-multiplexed SNP arrays: application to the large and unsequenced genomes of white spruce and black spruce. BMC Genomics 9(17p):21\"), [2012a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR73 \"Pavy N, Pelgas B, Laroche J, Rigault P, Isabel N, Bousquet J (2012a) A spruce gene map infers ancient plant genome reshuffling and subsequent slow evolution in the gymnosperm lineage leading to extant conifers. BMC Biol 10(18p):84\"), [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR76 \"Pavy N, Lamothe M, Pelgas B, Gagnon F, Birol I, Bohlmann J et al. (2017) A high-resolution reference genetic map positioning 8.8 K genes for the conifer white spruce: structural genomics implications and correspondence with physical distance. Plant J 90:189–203\"); Lind et al. [2014](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR56 \"Lind M, Källman T, Chen J, Ma X-F, Bousquet J, Morgante M et al. (2014) A Picea abies linkage map based on SNP markers identifies QTLs for four aspects of resistance to Heterobasidion parviporum infection. PLoS ONE 9(16p):e101049\"); Gagalova et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR30 \"Gagalova KK, Warren RL, Coombe L, Wong J, Nip KM, Yuen MM, Saint et al. (2022) Spruce giga-genomes: structurally similar yet distinctive with differentially expanding gene families and rapidly evolving genes. Plant J 111:1469–1485\")). This is likely to be the case for most tree species. It was further shown by Lenz et al. ([2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\")) that when limiting GS modeling to gene SNPs from one chromosome-wide linkage group at a time, model accuracy was below that obtained when considering the same number of SNPs but sampled from all 12 spruce chromosomes. It was suggested that relatedness could not be entirely captured when limiting marker sampling to one chromosome, or more likely, that short-range marker-QTL LD was significant but could not be entirely captured when marker distribution was skewed toward only a portion of the genome. Future efforts to design SNP genotyping arrays or to orient genotype-by-sequencing (GBS) strategies should thus consider not only the total number of SNPs required to optimize genome coverage and GS model performance, but also the distribution and number of distinct genetic loci sampled. A thoughtful selection of markers representative of a large array of genetic loci and thus, with a genome-wide or exome-wide distribution, should help improve the balance between genome coverage in markers, GS precision, and genotyping costs so as to optimize and make cost-effective the application of GS in a given breeding program.\n\nIt was further informed by Beaulieu et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")) that irrespective of tree species genome size in base pairs, which may vary by a factor of more than 50 times among tree species (from about 0.5 Gb to above 20 Gb), that the genome size in centimorgans (cM), thus based on recombination frequency, was of the same magnitude for most tree species so far targeted by GS. This suggests, for similar breeding population structures, that the marker density for most tree species should be in the same range, in order to reach plateaus of genetic parameter estimates and GS model accuracy. Thus, the optimal levels of marker density identified in the present study, in terms of the number of markers per cM of around two to four for spruce genomes of about 2000 cM (Beaulieu et al. [2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")), should be applicable to most conifer and broadleaf tree species in a similar range of genome size in cM. Such a number of markers and marker density is also well in agreement with early simulation results of Grattapaglia and Resende ([2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR38 \"Grattapaglia D, Resende MDV (2011) Genomic selection in forest tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 7:241–255\")) and the empirical results of Estopa et al. ([2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR28 \"Estopa RA, Paludeto JGZ, Müller BSF, de Oliveira RA, Azevedo CF, de Resende MDV et al. (2023) Genomic prediction of growth and wood quality traits in Eucalyptus benthamii using different genomic models and variable SNP genotyping density. New For 54:343–362\")) in *E. benthamii*, which is characterized by a much smaller physical genome than that of spruces or conifers in general, but of the same magnitude of genome size in cM (using *E. nitens* as a baseline reference for *Eucalyptus* sp., see Beaulieu et al. ([2024](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR7 \"Beaulieu J, Lenz PRN, Laverdière JP, Nadeau S, Bousquet J (2024) A meta-analysis on the effects of marker coverage, status number, and size of training set on predictive accuracy and heritability estimates from genomic selection in tree breeding. Tree Genet Genomes 20(14p):22\")), Table [1](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#Tab1)).\n\nPreselection of markers has also been reported to enhance GS model performance (Lenz et al. [2017](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR53 \"Lenz PRN, Beaulieu J, Mansfield SD, Clément S, Desponts M, Bousquet J (2017) Factors affecting the accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in an advanced-breeding population of black spruce (Picea mariana). BMC Genomics 18(17p):335\"); Chen et al. [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\"); Thumma et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR89 \"Thumma BR, Joyce KR, Jacobs A (2022) Genomic studies with preselected markers reveal dominance effects influencing growth traits in Eucalyptus nitens. G3 Genes Genomes Genet 12(14p):1\")), which could thus serve to enhance model accuracy when only the manufacture of small SNP arrays could be afforded, or to reduce the number of well-distributed markers needed to reach plateaus of model accuracy, given the type of breeding population targeted. Depending on the selection method, this could possibly shift the usual reliance of the GS model accuracy on relatedness to enhance the detection of marker-QTL LD.\n\nHigh LD has also been suggested to make GS model predictions more accurate and sustainable, not only across related populations, but also across generations (Habier et al. [2010](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR42 \"Habier D, Tetens J, Seefried FR, Lichtner P, Thaller G (2010) The impact of genetic relationship information on genomic breeding values in German Holstein cattle. Genet Sel Evol 42(12p):5\"), [2013](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR41 \"Habier D, Rohan LF, Dorian JG (2013) Genomic BLUP decoded: a look into the black box of genomic prediction. Genetics 194(3):597–607\")). In the context of long-term breeding programs, such as those in animal genetics or for forest trees, deeper pedigrees and accumulated recombination events could erode linkage between markers and QTLs, though this would less affect long-range LD underlying relatedness. Indeed, preliminary demonstrations in conifers are very encouraging when relatedness is maintained across generations (Bartholomé et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR3 \"Bartholomé J, Van Heerwaarden J, Isik F, Boury C, Vidal M, Plomion C et al. (2016) Performance of genomic prediction within and across generations in maritime pine. BMC Genomics 17(14p):604\")). This question may require more studies using haplotype-based approaches to efficiently capture and track structure across generations.\n\n### Breeding applications\n\nMost gains per unit of time and economic benefits in tree breeding could be accomplished by combining GS with multiclonal selection and deployment (Park et al. [2016](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR69 \"Park Y-S, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2016) Multi-varietal forestry integrating genomic selection and somatic embryogenesis. In: Park Y-S, Bonga J, Moon HK (eds) Vegetative propagation of forest trees. National Institute of Forest Science, Seoul, South Korea, pp 302–322\"); Chamberland et al. [2020](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR18 \"Chamberland V, Robichaud F, Perron M, Gélinas N, Bousquet J, Beaulieu J (2020) Conventional versus genomic selection for white spruce improvement: a comparison of costs and benefits of plantations on Quebec public lands. Tree Genet Genomes 16(16p):17\"); McLean et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR58 \"McLean D, Apiolaza L, Paget M, Klápště J (2023) Simulating deployment of genetic gain in a radiata pine breeding program with genomic selection. Tree Genet Genomes 19(16p):33\")) whenever possible, which is particularly amenable in tree species prone to vegetative propagation, such as spruces. With forward selection, the best clones can be selected within families for propagation when additive and dominance effects are well separated, as shown in this study; other non-additive effects, such as epistasis, could not be considered in the models because of the nature of the material used herein and therefore, remain unaccounted for. Currently, tree breeding programs rely mostly on additive genetic effects, though the situation is changing with the evolution towards more advanced-breeding generations in many tree breeding programs (Mullin et al. [2011](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR61 \"Mullin T, Andersson B, Bastien J-C, Beaulieu J, Burdon RD, Dvorak WS et al. (2011) Economic importance, breeding objectives and achievements. In: Plomion C, Bousquet J, Kole C (eds) Genetics, genomics and breeding of conifers. CRC Press & Science Publishers, New York, pp 40–127\"); Li and Dungey [2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR54 \"Li Y, Dungey HS (2018) Expected benefit of genomic selection over forward selection in conifer breeding and deployment. PLoS ONE 13(21p):0208232\"); Lenz et al. [2020a](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR52 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Josée M, Martin M, Isabel N, Beaulieu J, Bousquet J (2020a) Multi-trait genomic selection for weevil resistance, growth, and wood quality in Norway spruce. Evol Appl 13:76–94\"), [b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR51 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Azaiez A, Gérardi S, Deslauriers M, Perron M et al. (2020b) Genomic prediction for hastening and improving efficiency of forward selection in conifer polycross mating designs: an example from white spruce. Heredity 124:562–578\"); Grattapaglia et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR37 \"Grattapaglia D (2022) Twelve years into genomic selection in forest trees: climbing the slope of enlightenment of marker assisted tree breeding. Forests 13(25p):10\")).\n\nGiven that once manufactured, SNP genotyping arrays are reusable for many years and deliver stable genotyping results (Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\")), or that they could be designed efficiently for the needs of several closely-related species (Silva-Junior et al. [2015](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR85 \"Silva-Junior OB, Faria DA, Grattapaglia D (2015) A flexible multi-species genome-wide 60K chip developed from pooled resequencing of 240 Eucalyptus tree genomes across 12 species. N Phytol 206:1527–1540\"); Gérardi et al. [2025](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR33 \"Gérardi S, Gagnon F, Laroche J, Boyle B, Pavy N, Nadeau S, Soro A et al. (2025) Development and assessment of an exome-wide SNP genotyping array for GWAS, genomic prediction and assessment of introgressive hybridization between black and red spruces, and transferability to white and Norway spruces. bioRxiv \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.14.688480\n                  \n                \")), the popular belief is still that they remain too costly for tree breeding programs that often operate with limited budgets. However, one must also realize that the genotyping costs, including those for the manufacture of such SNP genotyping arrays, have decreased significantly since the introduction of GS in breeding (Gorjanc et al. [2015](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR36 \"Gorjanc G, Cleveland MA, Houston RD, Hickey JM (2015) Potential of genotyping-by-sequencing for genomic selection in livestock populations. Genet Sel Evol 47(13p):12\"); Bhandari et al. [2019](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR12 \"Bhandari A, Bartholomé J, Cao-Hamadoun TV, Kumari N, Frouin J, Kumar A et al. (2019) Selection of trait-specific markers and multi-environment models improve genomic predictive ability in rice. PLoS ONE 14(21p):0208871\")). Carefully designed lower-density SNP arrays should also provide a more affordable solution to tree breeders, with final genotyping cost per sample decreasing with an increase in the number of samples expected to be genotyped and thus, planning ahead for multi-year needs by the tree breeders.\n\nThe present study revealed that a well-distributed number of markers of around 4000 to 8000 SNPs for a genome size of around 2000 cM (corresponding to 2 to 4 SNPs/cM) resulted in nearly-maximum heritability estimates and GS model precision for both growth and wood quality traits. Our results indicate that additional genotyping costs aiming at further improving GS estimates beyond such a marker density may not be necessary, as additional gains will most likely be marginal even by doubling or tripling marker density. Consequently, such marker density for advanced-breeding populations of relatively small status number offers a fair trade-off between genotyping costs and the precision of genetic parameters and GS model accuracy obtained, making further investment in genotyping less justifiable. Beyond such a level of marker density, it is also more likely that improving the size of the training set in GS modeling (e.g., Nadeau et al. [2023](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR62 \"Nadeau S, Beaulieu J, Gezan SA, Perron M, Bousquet J, Lenz PRN (2023) Increasing genomic prediction accuracy for unphenotyped full- sib families by modeling additive and dominance effects with large datasets in white spruce. Front Plant Sci 14(24p):1137834\")) will be more productive than increasing the number of markers by a large factor. This is because of the presence of relatedness and high LD in advanced-breeding populations, which usually relies on controlled crosses or polycross (e.g., Lenz et al. [2020b](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR51 \"Lenz P, Nadeau S, Azaiez A, Gérardi S, Deslauriers M, Perron M et al. (2020b) Genomic prediction for hastening and improving efficiency of forward selection in conifer polycross mating designs: an example from white spruce. Heredity 124:562–578\")) of a quite limited number of selected parents, and the necessity of sampling well within families to maximize modeling accuracy, whether by conventional pedigree methods or by GS.\n\nThus, the remaining main obstacle to fully integrating GS into existing tree breeding programs does not appear technical anymore, but mostly of a strategic and financial nature. As emphasized by Grattapaglia et al. (2022) and several others, identifying well the objectives and understanding the logistic and financial dimensions of implementing GS in a given tree breeding program appears to be crucial for its adoption and success. A careful balance between genotyping costs and GS model accuracy should thus contribute to ensuring the long-term adoption of GS in tree breeding and hastening breeding cycles in the context of rapid environmental change (Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\"); Laverdière et al. [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR48 \"Laverdière J-P, Lenz P, Nadeau S, Depardieu C, Isabel N, Perron M et al. (2022) Breeding for adaptation to climate change: genomic selection for drought response in a white spruce multi-site polycross test. Evol Appl 15:383–402\")). As such, unconvinced tree breeders should have a second look at the costs and benefits of implementing GS in their breeding programs, especially with a vision of mid- to long-term needs and applications.\n\nConclusions\n-----------\n\nOur study tested varying marker density levels and their effects on the accuracy of GS models for traits of different genetic architecture in three boreal conifers widely reforested in the northern hemisphere. The results bear important implications for the operational deployment of GS in tree breeding programs beyond those of black spruce, white spruce, and Norway spruce. Despite some differences in experimental setups among the cases analysed herein, similar plateaus in heritability estimates and predictive ability of the GS models were observed between 4000 and 8000 SNPs, which were largely representing distinct gene loci well spread on the genome. This trend indicates that this range of relatively low marker density may offer a practical balance between GS accuracy and cost, although additional gains may still be achievable with larger numbers of markers in populations with much larger status numbers, but usually with diminishing rewards as genetic structure is reduced and status number increases. Given the high cost of manufacturing and using very large SNP arrays, particularly in tree breeding contexts where budgets are often much constrained, smaller exome-wide SNP panels designed to capture maximum genetic information per marker used should provide a cost-effective alternative, especially for applications in breeding populations of small status number where GS accuracy mostly relies on relatedness. Thus, the need for surveying very large number of SNPs, as usually needed for GWAS applications in unstructured or natural populations of conifers of large effective population size and low levels of LD, should certainly not be taken as a yardstick for GS applications, given the high recombination rates and rapid LD decay across many generations in these populations (Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR15 \"Bousquet J, Gérardi S, de Lafontaine G, Jaramillo-Correa JP, Pavy N, Prunier J et al. (2021) Spruce population genomics. In: Rajora OP (ed) Population genomics: forest trees. Springer Nature, Cham, Switzerland, pp. 1–64\")). This is contrary to advanced-breeding populations with usually much smaller status numbers and more relatedness, where GS should be most effectively applied, thus necessitating much less intense genome coverage.\n\nOur results also underscore the importance of considering the diversity of sampled genetic loci and their genomic distribution when designing genotyping arrays or for the GBS strategy. Because of SNPs generally being in high LD within genetic loci among related individuals, these results demonstrate the relevance of reducing SNP redundancy within loci and relying on relatively low numbers but well-distributed markers on the genome for predictive performance within a single generation of GS. Operational implementation of GS should thus aim for cost efficiency by maximising marker distribution on the genome for a given allocation of SNPs in genotyping strategies. As genotyping technologies have become more accessible and reliable (Bousquet et al. [2021](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR10 \"Bernhardsson C, Zan Y, Chen Z, Ingvarsson PK, Wu HX (2021) Development of a highly efficient 50K single nucleotide polymorphism genotyping array for the large and complex genome of Norway spruce (Picea abies L. Karst) by whole genome resequencing and its transferability to other spruce species. Mol Ecol Resour 21:880–896\"); Grattapaglia [2022](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR37 \"Grattapaglia D (2022) Twelve years into genomic selection in forest trees: climbing the slope of enlightenment of marker assisted tree breeding. Forests 13(25p):10\")), strategic planning and size of financial investment, rather than technical feasibility, are now representing the key factors determining the feasibility and successful routine integration of GS into forest tree breeding programs.\n\nData availability\n-----------------\n\nIn order to comply with Intellectual Property Policies (IPP) of participating governmental institutions in this work, the supporting phenotyping data for black spruce and white spruce is available at TreeSource upon request to the corresponding authors. The SNP genotyping datasets for the black spruce and white spruce components of the study are publicly available through the Federated Research Data Repository at <https://doi.org/10.20383/103.01620>. The SNP genotyping chip used to genotype black spruce and white spruce samples is described in Table S2 of Gérardi et al. ([2025](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR33 \"Gérardi S, Gagnon F, Laroche J, Boyle B, Pavy N, Nadeau S, Soro A et al. (2025) Development and assessment of an exome-wide SNP genotyping array for GWAS, genomic prediction and assessment of introgressive hybridization between black and red spruces, and transferability to white and Norway spruces. bioRxiv \n                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.14.688480\n                  \n                \"); <https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.11.14.688480>). For the Norway spruce component of the study, the phenotyping and genotyping datasets are those published in Chen et al. ([2018](/articles/s41437-026-00836-7#ref-CR21 \"Chen ZQ, Baison J, Pan J, Karlsson B, Andersson B, Westin J et al. (2018) Accuracy of genomic selection for growth and wood quality traits in two control-pollinated progeny trials using exome capture as the genotyping platform in Norway spruce. BMC Genomics 19(16p):946\")) and are available upon request to the corresponding author of that study.\n\nReferences\n----------\n\n* Amadeu RR, Cellon C, Olmstead JW, Garcia AAF, Resende MFR, Muñoz PR (2016) AGHmatrix: R package to construct relationship matrices for autotetraploid and diploid species: a blueberry example.