          "content": "A genomic prediction model is a statistical or machine learning model that uses genome-wide DNA marker data—typically thousands to millions of single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs)—to predict an individual's genetic value, trait performance, or disease risk without directly observing the trait. It works by learning marker–trait relationships from a training (reference) population of individuals who have both high-quality phenotypes and dense genome-wide genotypes, then applying those learned relationships to predict traits in new individuals [1]. In plant and animal breeding, the model converts a genome-wide SNP profile into a *genomic estimated breeding value* (GEBV)—an individual's predicted genetic merit for a target trait—enabling early selection of juvenile candidates, shortening breeding cycles, and accelerating genetic gain per year [1]. In human genetics, the equivalent is a *polygenic risk score* (PRS), which sums variants across the genome, each weighted by its effect size, to assess an individual's likelihood of developing conditions such as coronary artery disease or type 2 diabetes [2][3][4]. Common modeling approaches include genomic best linear unbiased prediction (GBLUP), Bayesian methods, and shrinkage techniques such as Lasso and ridge regression, with prediction accuracy—measured as the correlation between observed and predicted values or via metrics like AUC for disease risk—depending on trait heritability, training population size and diversity, marker density, relatedness between training and target individuals, and ancestry matching [1][3][5]. The concept traces to the foundational work of Meuwissen, Hayes, and Goddard (2001), who demonstrated that dense genome-wide marker maps could predict total genetic value, an approach later widely adopted in dairy cattle breeding and subsequently across crops and other livestock [1][6].\n\n### Sources",
